in to the point. It can be calculated using the formula:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0, 3)$, we have $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. So,\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. The angle $\\theta$ is the angle between the positive x-axis and the line segment connecting the origin to the point. It can be calculated using the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0, 3)$, we have $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. The value of $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{0}\\right)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ because the point lies on the positive y-axis.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $(0, 3)$ are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\n# Since x is 0, we know the angle is pi/2\ntheta = math.pi / 2\n\n# Display the polar coordinates\npolar_coordinates = (r, theta)\nprint(polar_coordinates)\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0, 3)$ are \\(\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the rectangular coordinates $(0, 3)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. The distance from the origin (radius $r$) is given by:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n2. The angle $\\theta$ is given by:\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\geq 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point $(0, 3)$:\n- The radius $r$ is:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n- The angle $\\theta$ is:\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\nsince $x = 0$ and $y > 0$.\n\nSo the polar coordinates are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\nif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\nelif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates for the point $(0, 3)$ are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the point $(0,3)$ from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of $r$ and $\\theta$. The formulas for converting from rectangular coordinates $(x, y)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ are:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point $(0,3)$, we can see that $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. Using the formulas above:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Let's confirm this with Python c