her complex number \\( c \\) by an angle of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) counter-clockwise. The steps to achieve this are as follows:\n\n1. Translate the complex plane so that \\( c \\) becomes the origin. This is done by subtracting \\( c \\) from \\( z \\).\n2. Rotate the resulting complex number by \\( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) counter-clockwise. This is done by multiplying by \\( e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}} = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + i\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + i\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\).\n3. Translate the complex plane back by adding \\( c \\) to the result of the rotation.\n\nLet's break it down step by step and implement it in Python using sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers z and c\nz = 2 + sp.sqrt(2) - (3 + 3*sp.sqrt(2))*sp.I\nc = 2 - 3*sp.I\n\n# Translate z so that c becomes the origin\nz_translated = z - c\n\n# Rotate z_translated by pi/4 counter-clockwise\nrotation_factor = sp.exp(sp.I * sp.pi / 4)\nz_rotated = z_translated * rotation_factor\n\n# Translate back by adding c\nw = z_rotated + c\n\n# Simplify the result\nw_simplified = sp.simplify(w)\nprint(w_simplified)\n```\n```output\n2 + (1 + I)*(-3*sqrt(2) - 3*I + sqrt(2) + 2*I)/sqrt(2) - 3*I\n```\nLet's simplify the expression further to get the final answer. The expression we obtained is:\n\n\\[ w = 2 + \\frac{(1 + i)(-3\\sqrt{2} - 3i + \\sqrt{2} + 2i)}{\\sqrt{2}} - 3i \\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the numerator:\n\n\\[ -3\\sqrt{2} - 3i + \\sqrt{2} + 2i = -2\\sqrt{2} - i \\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[ w = 2 + \\frac{(1 + i)(-2\\sqrt{2} - i)}{\\sqrt{2}} - 3i \\]\n\nNext, let's expand the numerator:\n\n\\[ (1 + i)(-2\\sqrt{2} - i) = -2\\sqrt{2} - i - 2\\sqrt{2}i - i^2 = -2\\sqrt{2} - i - 2\\sqrt{2}i + 1 = -2\\sqrt{2} + 1 - (1 + 2\\sqrt{2})i \\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[ w = 2 + \\frac{-2\\sqrt{2} + 1 - (1 + 2\\sqrt{2})i}{\\sqrt{2}} - 3i \\]\n\nNow, let's simplify the fraction:\n\n\\[ \\frac{-2\\sqrt{2} + 1 - (1 + 2\\sqrt