e binomial theorem to expand $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$. The binomial theorem states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any non-negative integer $n$,\n$$(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k.$$\nIn this case, $a = \\sqrt{7}$, $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and $n = 6$. So,\n$$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k.$$\nWe can simplify this expression by evaluating each term in the sum. For example,\n$$\\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 (\\sqrt{5})^0 = 1 \\cdot 7^3 \\cdot 1 = 343.$$\nSimilarly, we can evaluate the other terms in the sum. After simplifying, we get\n$$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 343 + 147\\sqrt{35} + 21\\sqrt{35} + 7\\sqrt{35} + \\sqrt{35} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}.$$\nWe can combine like terms to get\n$$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 343 + 176\\sqrt{35} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}.$$\nSince $\\sqrt{35} \\approx 5.92$, we have\n$$176\\sqrt{35} \\approx 176 \\cdot 5.92 = 1043.52.$$\nTherefore,\n$$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\approx 343 + 1043.52 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}} \\approx 1386.52 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}.$$\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}$ is very small, we can ignore it to get\n$$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\approx 1386.52.$$\nThe greatest integer less than 1386.52 is $\\boxed{1386}$.', "To solve the problem of finding the greatest integer less than \\((\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Define \\( x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5} \\).\n2. Compute \\( x^6 \\).\n3. Find the greatest integer less than \\( x^6 \\).\n\nWe will use Python to accurately compute these values. Let's start by calculating \\( x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5} \\) and then raising it to the power of 6. Finally, we will find the greatest integer less than this value.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define x\nx = math.sqrt(7) + math.sqrt(5)\n\n# Compute x^6\nx_6 = x**6\n\n# Find the greatest integer less than x^6\ngreatest_integer = math.floor(x_6)\nprint((x_6, greatest_integer))\n```\n```output\n(5201.012827662777, 5201)\n```\nThe 