exagon. Since the hexagon is regular, all its sides are equal, and the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{hex}}\\) of the hexagon is simply the side length multiplied by 6:\n\n\\[P_{\\text{hex}} = 6 \\times 16 \\text{ cm} = 96 \\text{ cm}\\]\n\nSince the octagon has the same perimeter as the hexagon, the perimeter of the octagon \\(P_{\\text{oct}}\\) is also 96 cm. A regular octagon has 8 equal sides, so if we let \\(s\\) be the side length of the octagon, then:\n\n\\[P_{\\text{oct}} = 8s\\]\n\nSetting \\(P_{\\text{oct}}\\) equal to 96 cm, we get:\n\n\\[8s = 96 \\text{ cm}\\]\n\nSolving for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[s = \\frac{96 \\text{ cm}}{8} = 12 \\text{ cm}\\]\n\nSo, the length of each side of the octagon is \\(12 \\text{ cm}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by writing and running the Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16  # in cm\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = 6 * side_length_hexagon\n\n# The perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_octagon = perimeter_hexagon\n\n# Calculate the side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_octagon / 8\n\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe length of each side of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.", "To solve the problem, we need to find the side length of a regular octagon that has the same perimeter as a regular hexagon with a side length of 16 cm.\n\n1. First, we calculate the perimeter of the hexagon. Since a hexagon has 6 sides, the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{hexagon}}\\) of the hexagon is:\n\\[ P_{\\text{hexagon}} = 6 \\times 16 \\text{ cm} = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n2. The perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon, so the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{octagon}}\\) of the octagon is also 96 cm. Since an octagon has 8 sides, if we let the side length of the octagon be \\(s\\), then:\n\\[ P_{\\text{octagon}} = 8 \\times s = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n3. Solving for \\(s\\), we get:\n\\[ s = \\frac{96 \\text{ cm}}{8} = 12 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSo, the side length of the octagon is 12 cm. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = 6 * side_length_hexagon\n\n# Since the perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon\n# Calculate the side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_hexagon / 8\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe side length of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.", 'To determine the side length of the regular octagon, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the perimeter of the regular hexagon.\n2. Use the perimeter of the hexagon to determine the side length of the regular octagon.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the Perimeter of the Hexagon\nA regular hexagon has 6 sides, and each side is 16 cm. Therefore, the perimeter \\( P_{\\text{hex}} \\) of the hexagon is:\n\\[ P_{\\text{hex}} = 6 \\times 16 \\text{ cm} = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Side Length of the Octagon\nA regular octagon has 8 sides. If the perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon (96 cm), then the side length \\( s_{\\text{oct}} \\) of the octagon can be found by dividing the perimeter by 8:\n\\[ s_{\\text{oct}} = \\frac{96 \\text{ cm}}{8} = 12 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSo, the side length of the regular octagon is \\( \\boxed{12} \\) cm.', "To solve the problem, we need to find the side length of a regular octagon that has the same perimeter as a regular hexagon with a side length of 16 cm.\n\n1. First, we calculate the perimeter of the hexagon. Since a hexagon has 6 sides, the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{hexagon}}\\) of the hexagon is:\n\\[ P_{\\text{hexagon}} = 6 \\times 16 \\text{ cm} = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n2. The perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon, so the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{octagon}}\\) of the octagon is also 96 cm. Since an octagon has 8 sides, the side length \\(s\\) of the octagon can be found by dividing the perimeter by 8:\n\\[ s = \\frac{P_{\\text{octagon}}}{8} = \\frac{96 \\text{ cm}}{8} = 12 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSo, the side length of the octagon is \\(12 \\text{ cm}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16\n\n# Number of sides in a hexagon\nnum_sides_hexagon = 6\n\n# Perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = side_length_hexagon * num_sides_hexagon\n\n# Number of sides in an octagon\nnum_sides_octagon = 8\n\n# Side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_hexagon / num_sides_octagon\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe side length of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.", "To find the side length of the regular octagon, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the perimeter of the regular hexagon.\n2. Since the octagon has the same perimeter as the hexagon, we can use this perimeter to find the side length of the octagon.\n\nA regular hexagon has 6 sides. If each side of the hexagon is 16 cm, then the perimeter of the hexagon is \\(6 \\times 16 = 96\\) cm.\n\nA regular octagon has 8 sides. If the perimeter of the octagon is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon (96 cm), then the side length of the octagon can be found by dividing the perimeter by 8. So, the side length of the octagon is \\( \\frac{96}{8} = 12 \\) cm.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16\n\n# Number of sides of the hexagon\nnum_sides_hexagon = 6\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = side_length_hexagon * num_sides_hexagon\n\n# Number of sides of the octagon\nnum_sides_octagon = 8\n\n# Calculate the side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_hexagon / num_sides_octagon\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe side length of each side of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the side length of a regular octagon that has the same perimeter as a regular hexagon with a side length of 16 cm.\n\n1. First, we calculate the perimeter of the hexagon. Since a hexagon has 6 sides, the perimeter \\(P_{\\text{hexagon}}\\) is:\n\\[ P_{\\text{hexagon}} = 6 \\times 16 = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n2. The perimeter of the octagon \\(P_{\\text{octagon}}\\) is the same as the perimeter of the hexagon, so:\n\\[ P_{\\text{octagon}} = 96 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\n3. Since an octagon has 8 sides, if the side length of the octagon is \\(s\\), then:\n\\[ 8 \\times s = 96 \\]\n\\[ s = \\frac{96}{8} = 12 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nSo, the side length of the octagon is 12 cm. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16\n\n# Number of sides of the hexagon\nnum_sides_hexagon = 6\n\n# Perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = num_sides_hexagon * side_length_hexagon\n\n# Number of sides of the octagon\nnum_sides_octagon = 8\n\n# Side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_hexagon / num_sides_octagon\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe side length of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.", "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the perimeter of the regular hexagon.\n2. Since the octagon has the same perimeter as the hexagon, use this perimeter to find the side length of the octagon.\n\nA regular hexagon has 6 sides, and each side is 16 cm. So, the perimeter of the hexagon is \\(6 \\times 16 = 96\\) cm.\n\nA regular octagon has 8 sides. If we let the side length of the octagon be \\(s\\), then the perimeter of the octagon is \\(8s\\). Since the perimeters are equal, we have:\n\\[8s = 96\\]\nSolving for \\(s\\):\n\\[s = \\frac{96}{8} = 12\\]\n\nSo, each side of the octagon is 12 cm. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given side length of the hexagon\nside_length_hexagon = 16\n\n# Number of sides of the hexagon\nnum_sides_hexagon = 6\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the hexagon\nperimeter_hexagon = side_length_hexagon * num_sides_hexagon\n\n# Number of sides of the octagon\nnum_sides_octagon = 8\n\n# Calculate the side length of the octagon\nside_length_octagon = perimeter_hexagon / num_sides_octagon\nprint(side_length_octagon)\n```\n```output\n12.0\n```\nThe side length of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cm.\n\nThe equation $x^2+12y+57=-y^2-10x$ represents a circle in 2D. Extend this to 3D space. Let $S$ be the sphere with an analogous equation $x^2+12y+57=-y^2-10x-z^2$. Find the center $(a, b, c)$ and the radius $r$ of the sphere, and calculate the value of $a + b + c + r$.\nTo find the center and the radius of the sphere, we first need to rewrite the given equation in the standard form of a sphere equation. The given equation is:\n\n\\[x^2 + 12y + 57 = -y^2 - 10x - z^2\\]\n\nFirst, let's rearrange all the terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[x^2 + 10x + y^2 + 12y + z^2 + 57 = 0\\]\n\nNext, we need to complete the square for the \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) terms. Let's complete the square for each variable:\n\n1. For \\(x\\):\n\\[x^2 + 10x = (x + 5)^2 - 25\\]\n\n2. For \\(y\\):\n\\[y^2 + 12y = (y + 6)^2 - 36\\]\n\n3. For \\(z\\):\n\\[z^2 = z^2\\]\n\nSubstituting these back into the equation, we get:\n\\[(x + 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 6)^2 - 36 + z^2 + 57 = 0\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants, we get:\n\\[(x + 5)^2 + (y + 6)^2 + z^2 - 4 = 0\\]\n\nRearranging gives us the standard form of the sphere equation:\n\\[(x + 5)^2 + (y + 6)^2 + z^2 = 4\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can see that the center of the sphere is \\((-5, -6, 0)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nNow, we need to calculate the value of \\(a + b + c + r\\), where \\((a, b, c)\\) is the center of the sphere and \\(r\\) is the radius. So, we have:\n\\[a = -5, \\quad b = -6, \\quad c = 0, \\quad r = 2\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(a + b + c + r = -5 + (-6) + 0 + 2 = -9\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Center of the sphere (a, b, c) and radius r\r\na = -5\r\nb = -6\r\nc = 0\r\nr = 2\r\n\r\n# Calculate the value of a + b + c + r\r\nresult = a + b + c + r\r\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n-9\n```\nThe value of \\(a + b + c + r\\) is \\(\\boxed{-9}\\).\n\nGiven the function $f(x) = 2\\cos(x)(\\sin(x) + \\cos(x))$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$,\n(Ⅰ) Find the smallest positive period of function $f(x)$;\n(Ⅱ) Find the intervals on which function $f(x)$ is monotonically increasing;\n(Ⅲ) Find the minimum and maximum values of function $f(x)$ on the interval $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]$.\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow the steps outlined. Let's break it down:\n\n1. **Find the smallest positive period of the function \\(f(x)\\)**.\n2. **Find the intervals on which the function \\(f(x)\\) is monotonically increasing**.\n3. **Find the minimum and maximum values of the function \\(f(x)\\) on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\)**.\n\n### Step 1: Find the smallest positive period of the function \\(f(x)\\)\n\nThe function \\(f(x) = 2\\cos(x)(\\sin(x) + \\cos(x))\\) can be simplified using trigonometric identities. We can use the double-angle identities and sum-to-product formulas to simplify the expression.\n\nFirst, let's simplify the function:\n\\[ f(x) = 2\\cos(x)\\sin(x) + 2\\cos^2(x) = \\sin(2x) + \\cos(2x) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1. \\]\n\nThe period of \\(\\sin\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\) is \\(\\pi\\), so the smallest positive period of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the intervals on which the function \\(f(x)\\) is monotonically increasing\n\nTo find the intervals where \\(f(x)\\) is increasing, we need to find the derivative of \\(f(x)\\) and determine where it is positive.\n\nThe derivative of \\(f(x)\\) is:\n\\[ f'(x) = 2\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right). \\]\n\nWe need to find the intervals where \\(f'(x) > 0\\):\n\\[ 2\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) > 0 \\implies \\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) > 0. \\]\n\nThe cosine function is positive in the intervals \\((-\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi)\\) for \\(k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\). So, for our function:\n\\[ -\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi < 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} < \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi. \\]\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\\[ -\\frac{3\\pi}{8} + k\\pi < x < \\frac{\\pi}{8} + k\\pi. \\]\n\nSo the intervals on which \\(f(x)\\) is monotonically increasing are:\n\\[ \\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{8} + k\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{8} + k\\pi\\right) \\text{ for } k \\in \\mathbb{Z}. \\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the minimum and maximum values of the function \\(f(x)\\) on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\)\n\nTo find the minimum and maximum values of \\(f(x)\\) on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\), we need to evaluate \\(f(x)\\) at the critical points within this interval and at the endpoints of the interval.\n\nThe critical points occur where \\(f'(x) = 0\\):\n\\[ \\cos\\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0. \\]\nThis occurs when:\n\\[ 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi \\implies x = \\frac{\\pi}{8} + \\frac{k\\pi}{2}. \\]\nThe only critical point in the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\) is \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{8}\\).\n\nNow, we evaluate \\(f(x)\\) at the endpoints and the critical point:\n\\[ f\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) + 1 = -1 + 1 = 0. \\]\n\\[ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. \\]\n\\[ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\right) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + 1 = \\sqrt{2}(1) + 1 = \\sqrt{2} + 1. \\]\n\nSo the minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\) is \\(0\\) and the maximum value is \\(\\sqrt{2} + 1\\).\n\nLet's summarize the results:\n\n1. The smallest positive period of the function \\(f(x)\\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n2. The intervals on which the function \\(f(x)\\) is monotonically increasing are \\(\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{8} + k\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{8} + k\\pi\\right)\\) for \\(k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\).\n3. The minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right]\\) is \\(0\\) and the maximum value is \\(\\sqrt{2} + 1\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi}{8} + k\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{8} + k\\pi\\right) \\text{ for } k \\in \\mathbb{Z}}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{0 \\text{ and } \\sqrt{2} + 1}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = 2 * sp.cos(x) * (sp.sin(x) + sp.cos(x))\n\n# Simplify the function\nf_simplified = sp.simplify(f)\nprint(f_simplified)\n\n# Find the period\nperiod = sp.periodicity(f_simplified, x)\nprint(period)\n\n# Find the derivative\nf_prime = sp.diff(f_simplified, x)\n\n# Find the critical points\ncritical_points = sp.solveset(f_prime, x, domain=sp.Interval(-sp.pi/4, sp.pi/4))\nprint(critical_points)\n\n# Evaluate the function at the endpoints and critical points\nf_at_neg_pi_over_4 = f_simplified.subs(x, -sp.pi/4)\nf_at_pi_over_4 = f_simplified.subs(x, sp.pi/4)\nf_at_critical_point = f_simplified.subs(x, sp.pi/8)\n\nprint(f_at_neg_pi_over_4)\nprint(f_at_pi_over_4)\nprint(f_at_critical_point)\n```\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is an even function defined on $\\mathbb{R}$, and $g(x)$ is an odd function defined on $\\mathbb{R}$, and $g(x) = f(x-1)$, then 