e = 2  # relative to adjacent\nadjacent = 1  # relative to adjacent\n\ntan_A = opposite / adjacent\nprint(tan_A)\n```\n```output\n2.0\n```\nThe value of $\\tan A$ is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To find $\\tan A$ in the right triangle $ABC$ with $\\angle B = 90^\\circ$ and $\\sin A = 2\\cos A$, we can use the definitions of the trigonometric functions in terms of the sides of the triangle.\n\n1. Let's denote the sides of the triangle as follows:\n   - $a$ is the length of the side opposite to $\\angle A$.\n   - $b$ is the length of the side adjacent to $\\angle A$.\n   - $c$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\n2. By definition, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sin A = \\frac{a}{c} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos A = \\frac{b}{c}\n   \\]\n   Given that $\\sin A = 2\\cos A$, we can substitute these definitions in:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a}{c} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = 2b\n   \\]\n\n3. The tangent of $\\angle A$ is defined as:\n   \\[\n   \\tan A = \\frac{a}{b}\n   \\]\n   Substituting $a = 2b$ into the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\tan A = \\frac{2b}{b} = 2\n   \\]\n\nSo, the value of $\\tan A$ is $\\boxed{2}$.", 'Given that $\\sin A = 2\\cos A$ in a right triangle $ABC$ with $\\angle B = 90^\\circ$, we can use the definitions of sine and cosine in a right triangle to find $\\tan A$.\n\nIn a right triangle, $\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$ and $\\cos A = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos A = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n\\]\nGiven that $\\sin A = 2\\cos A$, we can write:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\text{opposite} = 2 \\cdot \\text{adjacent}\n\\]\nThe tangent of an angle in a right triangle is given by $\\tan A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}$. Using the 