inimum value of 8 at \\(x = 2\\) and the line \\(y = 8\\) intersects both parts of the function, the largest value of \\(a\\) is indeed 2.\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To determine the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, we need to analyze the behavior of the function \\(f(x)\\).\n\nThe function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nax^2 & \\text{if } x \\geq a, \\\\\nax + 2a & \\text{if } x < a.\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor \\(f(x)\\) to intersect every horizontal line at least once, it must take on all possible \\(y\\)-values. This means that the function must be surjective (i.e., its range must be all real numbers).\n\nLet's analyze the two pieces of the function separately:\n\n1. For \\(x \\geq a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax^2\\). This is a parabola that opens upwards if \\(a > 0\\) and downwards if \\(a < 0\\). The minimum value of this part of the function occurs at \\(x = a\\), which gives \\(f(a) = a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\).\n\n2. For \\(x < a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\). This is a linear function. As \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), \\(f(x)\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\) if \\(a > 0\\) and \\(\\infty\\) if \\(a < 0\\). As \\(x\\) approaches \\(a\\) from the left, \\(f(x)\\) approaches \\(a \\cdot a + 2a = a^2 + 2a\\).\n\nTo ensure that \\(f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, the range of the function must include all real numbers. This means that the minimum value of the parabola \\(a^3\\) must be less than or equal to the minimum value of the linear function as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), and the maximum value of the linear function as \\(x\\) approaches \\(a\\) from the left must be greater than or equal to the maximum value of the parabola as \\(x\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\).\n\nFor \\(a > 0\\), the parabola \\(ax^2\\) has a minimum value of \\(a^3\\) at \\(x = a\\), and the linear function \\(ax + 2a\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\). However, for \\(f(x)\\) to be surjective, the value \\(a^3\\) must be less than or equal to the value of the linear function as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), which is \\(-\\infty\\). This is not possible for \\(a > 0\\).\n\nFor \\(a < 0\\), the parabola \\(ax^2\\) has a maximum value of \\(a^3\\) at \\(x = a\\), and the linear function \\(ax + 2a\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\). For \\(f(x)\\) to be surjective, the value \\(a^3\\) must be greater than or equal to the value of the linear function as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), which is \\(-\\infty\\). This is possible for \\(a < 0\\).\n\nThe critical value occurs when the minimum value of the parabola \\(a^3\\) is equal to the value of the linear function as \\(x\\) approaches \\(a\\) from the left, which is \\(a^2 + 2a\\). So we need to solve the equation:\n\\[ a^3 = a^2 + 2a. \\]\n\nLet's solve this equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[ a^3 - a^2 - 2a = 0, \\]\n\\[ a(a^2 - a - 2) = 0. \\]\n\nThis gives us the solutions:\n\\[ a = 0, \\]\n\\[ a^2 - a - 2 = 0. \\]\n\nThe quadratic equation \\(a^2 - a - 2 = 0\\) can be factored as:\n\\[ (a - 2)(a + 1) = 0. \\]\n\nSo the solutions are:\n\\[ a = 2, \\]\n\\[ a = -1. \\]\n\nSince we are looking for the largest value of \\(a\\) such that \\(a < 0\\), the solution is \\(a = -1\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution by checking the values of the function at the critical points. For \\(a = -1\\), the function is:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \n-x^2 & \\text{if } x \\geq -1, \\\\\n-x + 2 & \\text{if } x < -1.\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor \\(x \\geq -1\\), the minimum value of \\(f(x) = -x^2\\) is \\(-1\\) at \\(x = -1\\).\nFor \\(x < -1\\), the value of \\(f(x) = -x + 2\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), and at \\(x = -1\\), \\(f(x) = -(-1) + 2 = 3\\).\n\nSince the range of the function includes all real numbers, the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once is \\(a = -1\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once. This means that for any value of \\(y\\), there must be at least one \\(x\\) such that \\(f(x) = y\\).\n\nLet's analyze the function \\(f(x)\\) in two parts:\n\n1. For \\(x \\geq a\\), \\(f(x) = ax^2\\).\n2. For \\(x < a\\), \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\).\n\nWe need to ensure that the range of \\(f(x)\\) covers all real numbers. Let's consider the ranges of each part of the function:\n\n1. For \\(x \\geq a\\), the function \\(f(x) = ax^2\\) is a parabola that opens upwards. The minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) occurs at \\(x = a\\), and it is \\(f(a) = a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\). So, for \\(x \\geq a\\), the range of \\(f(x)\\) is \\([a^3, \\infty)\\).\n\n2. For \\(x < a\\), the function \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\) is a line with a slope of \\(a\\). As \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), \\(f(x)\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\) if \\(a > 0\\). The value of \\(f(x)\\) at \\(x = a\\) is \\(f(a) = a \\cdot a + 2a = a^2 + 2a\\). So, for \\(x < a\\), the range of \\(f(x)\\) is \\((-\\infty, a^2 + 2a)\\).\n\nFor the function \\(f(x)\\) to intersect every horizontal line at least once, the range of the function must cover all real numbers. This means that the upper bound of the range for \\(x < a\\) must be at least as large as the lower bound of the range for \\(x \\geq a\\). In other words, we need \\(a^2 + 2a \\geq a^3\\).\n\nLet's solve the inequality \\(a^2 + 2a \\geq a^3\\):\n\n\\[a^3 - a^2 - 2a \\leq 0\\]\n\\[a(a^2 - a - 2) \\leq 0\\]\n\\[a(a - 2)(a + 1) \\leq 0\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1) = 0\\) are \\(a = 0\\), \\(a = 2\\), and \\(a = -1\\). We need to determine the intervals where the