 vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe projection is:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u} = \\frac{5}{6} \\mathbf{u}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{u} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\\), we can equate the scalar multiples:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we compute the dot products:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 2 \\cdot 1 + y \\cdot (-2) + (-5) \\cdot 1 = 2 - 2y - 5 = -2y - 3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{-2y - 3}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-2y - 3 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n-2y = 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by plugging \\( y = -4 \\) back into the projection formula to ensure it matches the given projection.\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 2 \\cdot 1 + (-4) \\cdot (-2) + (-5) \\cdot 1 = 2 + 8 - 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis confirms that the projection is correct. Therefore, the value of \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", "To solve for \\( y \\), we need to use the formula for the projection of one vector onto another. The projection of vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) onto vector \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThe projection is:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis means:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{b} = \\frac{5}{6} \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\\), we can equate the scalar multiples:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\nFirst, compute \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\cdot 1 + y \\cdot (-2) + (-5) \\cdot 1 = 2 - 2y - 5 = -3 - 2y\n\\]\nNext, compute \\(\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-3 - 2y = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n-2y = 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\nLet's verify this by plugging \\( y = -4 \\) back into the projection formula to ensure it matches the given projection. We'll use Python to confirm the result.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\na = np.array([2, -4, -5])\nb = np.array([1, -2, 1])\n\n# Compute the dot products\ndot_ab = np.dot(a, b)\ndot_bb = np.dot(b, b)\n\n# Compute the projection\nprojection = (dot_ab / dot_bb) * b\n\n# Expected projection\nexpected_projection = (5 / 6) * b\n\n# Check if they are equal\nprint((projection, expected_projection, np.allclose(projection, expected_projection)))\n```\n```output\n(array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]), array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]), True)\n```\nThe computed projection \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.83333333 \\\\ -1.66666667 \\\\ 0.83333333 \\end{pmatrix}\\) matches the expected projection \\(\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0.83333333 \\\\ -1.66666667 \\\\ 0.83333333 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This confirms that our solution is correct.\n\nThus, the value of \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", "To find the value of \\( y \\), we need to use the formula for the projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot y + 1 \\cdot (-5) = 2 - 2y - 5 = -3 - 2y\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the projection formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe know that this projection is equal to \\(\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore, we can set the scalar coefficients equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n-3 - 2y = 5 \\implies -2y = 8 \\implies y = -4\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by substituting \\( y = -4 \\) back into the projection formula to ensure it matches the given projection. We'll use Python to confirm our result.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\nv = np.array([2, -4, -5])\nu = np.array([1, -2, 1])\n\n# Calculate the dot product u . v\ndot_product_uv = np.dot(u, v)\n\n# Calculate the dot product u . u\ndot_product_uu = np.dot(u, u)\n\n# Calculate the projection\nprojection = (dot_product_uv / dot_product_uu) * u\n\n# Given projection\ngiven_projection = (5/6