hat the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation are $\\lambda = 2$ and $\\lambda = 3$. These are the values for which there exists an $x \\in [0, 1]$ such that $f(x) \\neq x$ but $f(f(x)) = x$.\n\nTherefore, the values of $\\lambda$ are $\\boxed{2, 3}$.", "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection of the two given curves and then calculate the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\).\n\nThe equations of the curves are:\n1. \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\)\n2. \\(x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0\\)\n\nFirst, we will find the points of intersection by solving these equations simultaneously. We can do this by subtracting the second equation from the first to eliminate the \\(x^2\\) and \\(6x\\) terms:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72) - (x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0\n\\]\n\nDividing through by 2, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^2 - 20y + 59 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -20\\), and \\(c = 59\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{400 - 236}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{164}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm 2\\sqrt{41}}{2} = 10 \\pm \\sqrt{41}\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\) and \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\).\n\nNext, we substitute these \\(y\\)-values back into one of the original equations to find the corresponding \\(x\\)-coordinates. We'll use the first equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\):\n\nFor \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 - \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 - 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 + 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 + 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve these quadratic equations for \\(x\\) using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 \\mp 4\\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 \\pm 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 \\pm 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 \\pm 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 \\pm \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 \\pm \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\), \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\), \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\\), and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nThe intersection points are therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\) and sum them up. The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\nLet's calculate these distances and sum them up using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Intersection points\npoints = [\n    (-3 + 2 * math.sqrt(9 + math.sqrt(41)), 10 + math.sqrt(41)),\n    (-3 - 2 * math.sqrt(9 + math.sqrt(41)), 10 + math.sqrt(41)),\n    (-3 + 2 * math.sqrt(9 - math.sqrt(41)), 10 - math.sqrt(41)),\n    (-3 - 2 * math.sqrt(9 - math.sqrt(41)), 10 - math.sqrt(41))\n]\n\n# Point to which we are calculating the distances\npoint = (-3, 2)\n\n# Calculate the distances\ndistances = [math.sqrt((x - point[0])**2 + (y - point[1])**2) for x, y in points]\n\n# Sum of the distances\nsum_distances = sum(distances