ion points to the point $(-3, 2)$.\n4. Sum these distances.\n\nThe equations of the curves are:\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0 \\]\n\nFirst, we can simplify the equations by completing the square. Let's start with the first equation:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x + y^2 - 24y + 72 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 - 9 + (y - 12)^2 - 144 + 72 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + (y - 12)^2 = 72 + 9 + 144 = 225 \\]\nSo, the first equation represents a circle with center $(-3, 12)$ and radius $15$.\n\nNext, we simplify the second equation:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x - y^2 + 16y - 46 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 - 9 - (y - 8)^2 + 64 - 46 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 - (y - 8)^2 = 9 + 46 - 64 = -9 \\]\nSo, the second equation represents a hyperbola with center $(-3, 8)$.\n\nTo find the points of intersection, we can subtract the second equation from the first:\n\\[ (x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72) - (x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46) = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0 \\]\n\\[ y^2 - 20y + 59 = 0 \\]\nWe can solve this quadratic equation for $y$:\n\\[ y = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{400 - 236}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{164}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm 2\\sqrt{41}}{2} = 10 \\pm \\sqrt{41} \\]\nSo, the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points are $10 + \\sqrt{41}$ and $10 - \\sqrt{41}$. Substituting these back into the equation of the circle will give us the corresponding $x$-coordinates.\n\nLet's substitute $y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}$ into the circle equation:\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 12)^2 = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + (\\sqrt{41} - 2)^2 = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + 41 + 4 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 = 225 - 45 + 4\\sqrt{41} = 180 + 4\\sqrt{41} \\]\n\\[ x + 3 = \\pm \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n\\[ x = -3 \\pm \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}} \\]\nSimilarly, for $y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}$:\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 12)^2 = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + (-\\sqrt{41} - 2)^2 = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 + 41 + 4 + 4\\sqrt{41} = 225 \\]\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 = 225 - 45 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 180 - 4\\sqrt{41} \\]\n\\[ x + 3 = \\pm \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n\\[ x = -3 \\pm \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}} \\]\nSo, the intersection points are:\n\\[ (-3 + \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}) \\]\n\\[ (-3 - \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}) \\]\n\\[ (-3 + \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}) \\]\n\\[ (-3 - \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}) \\]\nNext, we need to calculate the distances from these points to the point $(-3, 2)$. The distance formula is:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2} \\]\nLet's calculate the distances for each point:\n1. For $(-3 + \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})$:\n\\[ d_1 = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}})^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41} + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} \\]\n\\[ d_1 = \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{285 + 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n2. For $(-3 - \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41})$:\n\\[ d_2 = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41}})^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41} + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} \\]\n\\[ d_2 = \\sqrt{180 + 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{285 + 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n3. For $(-3 + \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})$:\n\\[ d_3 = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}})^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41} + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} \\]\n\\[ d_3 = \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 - 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{285 - 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n4. For $(-3 - \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})$:\n\\[ d_4 = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41}})^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41} + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} \\]\n\\[ d_4 = \\sqrt{180 - 4\\sqrt{41} + 64 - 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{285 - 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\nFinally, we sum these distances:\n\\[ d_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 = \\sqrt{285 + 20\\sqrt{41}} + \\sqrt{285 + 20\\sqrt{41}} + \\sqrt{285 - 20\\sqrt{41}} + \\sqrt{285 - 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\n\\[ = 2\\sqrt{285 + 20\\sqrt{41}} + 2\\sqrt{285 - 20\\sqrt{41}} \\]\nLet's calculate this sum using Python to ensure accuracy