sponding \\(x\\)-values. Using the first equation:\n\\[x^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\\]\n\\[x^2 + 100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\\]\n\\[x^2 + 6x - 27 + 6\\sqrt{41} - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\\]\n\\[x^2 + 6x - 27 + 2\\sqrt{41} = 0\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(x\\). We can solve it similarly to find the \\(x\\)-coordinates. The solutions will be symmetric because the equations are symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis. So, the points of intersection are:\n\\[\n\\left(-3 + \\sqrt{41}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - \\sqrt{41}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 + \\sqrt{41}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - \\sqrt{41}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\\[\nd_1 = \\sqrt{((-3 + \\sqrt{41}) + 3)^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{41 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{41 + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{146 + 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\\[\nd_2 = \\sqrt{((-3 - \\sqrt{41}) + 3)^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{41 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{41 + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{146 + 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\\[\nd_3 = \\sqrt{((-3 + \\sqrt{41}) + 3)^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{41 + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{41 + 64 - 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{146 - 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\\[\nd_4 = \\sqrt{((-3 - \\sqrt{41}) + 3)^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{41 + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{41 + 64 - 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{146 - 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the distances is:\n\\[\nd_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 = 2\\sqrt{146 + 16\\sqrt{41}} + 2\\sqrt{146 - 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify this expression using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the expressions for the distances\r\nd1 = sp.sqrt(146 + 16 * sp.sqrt(41))\r\nd2 = sp.sqrt(146 - 16 * sp.sqrt(41))\r\n\r\n# Sum of the distances\r\nsum_distances = 2 * (d1 + d2)\r\nprint(sum_distances.evalf())\n```\n```output\n48.000000000000