\\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}.', "To solve the equation \\(|x+5| - |3x-6| = 0\\), we need to consider the points where the expressions inside the absolute values change their signs, i.e., \\(x = -5\\) and \\(x = 2\\). These points divide the real number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, -5)\\), \\([-5, 2)\\), and \\([2, \\infty)\\). We will solve the equation in each of these intervals separately.\n\n1. For \\(x < -5\\):\n   \\[\n   |x+5| = -(x+5) = -x-5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |3x-6| = -(3x-6) = -3x+6\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   -x-5 - (-3x+6) = 0 \\implies -x-5 + 3x-6 = 0 \\implies 2x-11 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{11}{2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{11}{2} \\not< -5\\), this solution is not valid in this interval.\n\n2. For \\(-5 \\leq x < 2\\):\n   \\[\n   |x+5| = x+5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |3x-6| = -(3x-6) = -3x+6\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   x+5 - (-3x+6) = 0 \\implies x+5 + 3x-6 = 0 \\implies 4x-1 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(-5 \\leq \\frac{1}{4} < 2\\), this solution is valid in this interval.\n\n3. For \\(x \\geq 2\\):\n   \\[\n   |x+5| = x+5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad |3x-6| = 3x-6\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   x+5 - (3x-6) = 0 \\implies x+5 - 3x+6 = 0 \\implies -2x+11 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{11}{2}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\frac{11}{2} \\geq 2\\), this solution is valid in this interval.\n\nThe valid solutions are \\(x = \\frac{1}{4}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{11}{2}\\). The largest possible value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\frac{11}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, Abs\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation\r\nequation = Eq(Abs(x + 5) - Abs(3*x - 6), 0)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equation\r\nsolutions = solve(equation, x)\r\nprint(solutions)\n```", 'The expression $|x+5|-|3x-6|$ can be split into different cases based on the values of $x$:\nCase 1: $x \\geq 2$\nIn this case, $|x+5|=x+5$ and $|3x-6|=3x-6$, so the equation becomes:\n$x+5-(3x-6)=0$\n$-2x+11=0$\n$x=\\frac{11}{2}$\nSince $x \\geq 2$, this solution is valid.\nCase 2: $-5 \\leq x < 2$\nIn this case, $|x+5|=x+5$ and $|3x-6|=6-3x$, so the equation becomes:\n$x+5-(6-3x)=0$\n$4x-1=0$\n$x=\\frac{1}{4}$\nSince $-5 \\leq x < 2$, this solution is valid.\nCase 3: $x < -5$\nIn this case, $|x+5|=-x-5$ and $|3x-6|=6-3x$, so the equation becomes:\n$-x-5-(6-3x)=0$\n$2x-11=0$\n$x=\\frac{11}{2}$\nHowever, this solution is not valid since $x < -5$.\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $x$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}}$.', "If $|x+5|-|3x-6|=0$, find the smallest possible value of $x$. Express your answer as an improper fraction. \\boxed{}. \\mathbb{Q}\nTo solve the equation \\( |x+5| - |3x-6| = 0 \\), we need to consider the points where the expressions inside the absolute values change their signs, i.e., \\( x = -5 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). These points divide the number line into three intervals: \\( (-\\infty, -5) \\), \\( (-5, 2) \\), and \\( (2, \\infty) \\). We will analyze the equation in each of these intervals.\n\n1. For \\( x < -5 \\):\n\\[ |x+5| = -(x+5) = -x-5 \\]\n\\[ |3x-6| = -(3x-6) = -3x+6 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ -x-5 - (-3x+6) = 0 \\]\n\\[ -x-5 + 3x - 6 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2x - 11 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11}{2} \\]\nSince \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\) is not in the interval \\( (-\\infty, -5) \\), we discard this solution.\n\n2. For \\( -5 \\le x < 2 \\):\n\\[ |x+5| = x+5 \\]\n\\[ |3x-6| = -(3x-6) = -3x+6 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ x+5 - (-3x+6) = 0 \\]\n\\[ x+5 + 3x - 6 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 4x - 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\nSince \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\) is in the interval \\( [-5, 2) \\), we keep this solution.\n\n3. For \\( x \\ge 2 \\):\n\\[ |x+5| = x+5 \\]\n\\[ |3x-6| = 3x-6 \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ x+5 - (3x-6) = 0 \\]\n\\[ x+5 - 3x + 6 = 0 \\]\n\\[ -2x + 11 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11}{2} \\]\nSince \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\) is in the interval \\( [2, \\infty) \\), we keep this solution.\n\nSo the solutions to the equation are \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\). The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\) and the smallest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\).\n\nLet's summarize the results:\n- The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}} \\).\n- The smallest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\).", 'If $|a-4|+|2+b|=0$, find $a+b$. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \