if there is a simpler pattern by calculating a few more terms using Python.\n\nWe'll write a Python function to iterate the function \\( F(z) \\) and observe the sequence.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the function F(z)\r\ndef F(z):\r\n    return (z + 1j) / (z - 1j)\r\n\r\n# Initial value z0\r\nz0 = 1/137 + 1j\r\n\r\n# Calculate the first few terms of the sequence\r\nz1 = F(z0)\r\nz2 = F(z1)\r\nz3 = F(z2)\r\nz4 = F(z3)\r\nz5 = F(z4)\r\nz6 = F(z5)\r\nz7 = F(z6)\r\nz8 = F(z7)\r\nz9 = F(z8)\r\nz10 = F(z9)\r\n\r\nprint((z0, z1, z2, z3, z4, z5, z6, z7, z8, z9, z10))\n```\n```output\n((0.0072992700729927005+1j), (1+274j), (1.0215085550458032+0.007272727272727273j), (0.9999999999999999+1j), (1+274j), (1.0215085550458032+0.007272727272727273j), (0.9999999999999999+1j), (1+274j), (1.0215085550458032+0.007272727272727273j), (0.9999999999999999+1j), (1+274j))\n```\nFrom the calculations, we can see that the sequence \\( z_n \\) appears to be periodic with a period of 3. Specifically, we have:\n\n- \\( z_0 = \\frac{1}{137} + i \\)\n- \\( z_1 = 1 + 274i \\)\n- \\( z_2 = 1.0215085550458032 + 0.007272727272727273i \\)\n- \\( z_3 = 1 + i \\)\n- \\( z_4 = 1 + 274i \\)\n- \\( z_5 = 1.0215085550458032 + 0.007272727272727273i \\)\n- \\( z_6 = 1 + i \\)\n- \\( z_7 = 1 + 274i \\)\n- \\( z_8 = 1.0215085550458032 + 0.007272727272727273i \\)\n- \\( z_9 = 1 + i \\)\n- \\( z_{10} = 1 + 274i \\)\n\nSince the sequence is periodic with a period of 3, we can find \\( z_{2002} \\) by determining the position of 2002 in the cycle. Specifically, we need to find the remainder when 2002 is divided by 3:\n\n\\[ 2002 \\mod 3 = 1 \\]\n\nSo, \\( z_{2002} = z_1 = 1 + 274i \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 + 274i}\n\\]", 'To convert the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) to base \\(5\\), we need to repeatedly divide the number by \\(5\\) and keep track of the remainders. The conversion process involves the following steps:\n\n1. Divide the number by \\(5\\).\n2. Record the remainder.\n3. Update the number to the quotient from the division.\n4. Repeat the process until the quotient is \\(0\\).\n5. The base \\(5\\) representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\nLet\'s perform this conversion using Python code.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_5_digits = []\n    \n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 5\n        base_5_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 5\n    \n    # The base 5 representation is the reverse of the collected remainders\n    base_5_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_5_digits)\n\n# Convert 555 from base 10 to base 5\ndecimal_number = 555\nbase_5_representation = decimal_to_base_5(decimal_number)\nprint(base_5_representation)\n```\n```output\n4210\n```\nThe base \\(5\\) representation of the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4210_5}\\).', 'To convert the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) to base \\(5\\), we need to repeatedly divide the number by \\(5\\) and keep track of the remainders. The conversion process involves the following steps:\n\n1. Divide the number by \\(5\\).\n2. Record the remainder.\n3. Update the number to the quotient from the division.\n4. Repeat steps 1-3 until the quotient is \\(0\\).\n5. The base \\(5\\) representation is the sequence of remainders read in reverse order.\n\nLet\'s perform these steps using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_5_digits = []\n    \n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 5\n        base_5_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 5\n    \n    # The base 5 representation is the reverse of the collected remainders\n    base_5_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_5_digits)\n\n# Convert 555 from base 10 to base 5\ndecimal_number = 555\nbase_5_representation = decimal_to_base_5(decimal_number)\nprint(base_5_representation)\n```\n```output\n4210\n```\nThe base \\(5\\) representation of the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4210_5}\\).', 'To convert a decimal number to a different base, we repeatedly divide the number by the new base and keep track of the remainders. The conversion process for $555_{10}$ to base $5$ is as follows:\n\n1. Divide 555 by 5, quotient is 111, remainder is 0.\n2. Divide 111 by 5, quotient is 22, remainder is 1.\n3. Divide 22 by 5, quotient is 4, remainder is 2.\n4. Divide 4 by 5, quotient is 0, remainder is 4.\n\nThe base 5 representation is obtained by reading the remainders from bottom to top. So, $555_{10}$ in base 5 is $4210_5$.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python to confirm the result.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base(n, base):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % base))\n        n //= base\n    return \'\'.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 555 from base 10 to base 5\ndecimal_number = 555\nbase = 5\nbase_5_representation = decimal_to_base(decimal_number, base)\nprint(base_5_representation)\n```\n```output\n4210\n```\nThe base 5 representation of $555_{10}$ is $\\boxed{4210_5}$.', 'To convert the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) to base \\(5\\), we need to repeatedly divide the number by \\(5\\) and keep track of the remainders. The conversion process involves the following steps:\n\n1. Divide the number by \\(5\\).\n2. Record the remainder.\n3. Update the number to the quotient of the division.\n4. Repeat steps 1-3 until the quotient is \\(0\\).\n5. The base \\(5\\) representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\nLet\'s perform this conversion using Python code.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_5_digits = []\n    \n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 5\n        base_5_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 5\n    \n    # The base 5 representation is the reverse of the collected remainders\n    base_5_digits.reverse()\n    return 