(remainder))\n        n = n // 5\n    \n    # The base 5 number is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top\n    base_5_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_5_digits)\n\n# Convert 555 from base 10 to base 5\nbase_5_number = decimal_to_base_5(555)\nprint(base_5_number)\n```\n```output\n4210\n```\nThe decimal number $555_{10}$ expressed in base 5 is $\\boxed{4210_5}$.', 'To convert the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) to base \\(5\\), we need to repeatedly divide the number by \\(5\\) and keep track of the remainders. The conversion process involves the following steps:\n\n1. Divide the number by \\(5\\).\n2. Record the remainder.\n3. Update the number to the quotient from the division.\n4. Repeat steps 1-3 until the quotient is \\(0\\).\n5. The base \\(5\\) representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\nLet\'s perform this conversion using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_5_digits = []\n    \n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 5\n        base_5_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 5\n    \n    # The base 5 representation is the reverse of the collected remainders\n    base_5_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_5_digits)\n\n# Convert 555 from base 10 to base 5\ndecimal_number = 555\nbase_5_representation = decimal_to_base_5(decimal_number)\nprint(base_5_representation)\n```\n```output\n4210\n```\nThe base \\(5\\) representation of the decimal number \\(555_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4210_5}\\).', 'To determine the fewest number of miles Suzanne can walk in February, we need to consider the following:\n1. February can have either 28 or 29 days depending on whether it is a leap year or not.\n2. Suzanne walks four miles every third day.\n\nFirst, we need to determine the number of days in February. Since the problem does not specify whether it is a leap year or not, we will consider both cases.\n\n### Case 1: February has 28 days\nIf February has 28 days, then we need to find out how many times Suzanne walks in 28 days. Since she walks every third day, we need to find the number of multiples of 3 that are less than or equal to 28. This can be calculated as:\n\\[ \\text{Number of walks} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{28}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 9 \\]\nSo, Suzanne walks 4 miles each time, so the total miles walked in February is:\n\\[ \\text{Total miles} = 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\]\n\n### Case 2: February has 29 days\nIf February has 29 days, then we need to find out how many times Suzanne walks in 29 days. Since she walks every third day, we need to find the number of multiples of 3 that are less than or equal to 29. This can be calculated as:\n\\[ \\text{Number of walks} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{29}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 9 \\]\nSo, Suzanne walks 4 miles each time, so the total miles walked in February is:\n\\[ \\text{Total miles} = 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\]\n\nIn both cases, the total miles walked in February is 36. Therefore, the fewest number of miles she can walk in February is 36.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]\n```python\n# Function to calculate the total miles walked in February\ndef calculate_miles(days_in_february):\n    # Number of walks in February\n    number_of_walks = days_in_february // 3\n    # Total miles walked\n    total_miles = number_of_walks * 4\n    return total_miles\n\n# Calculate for both cases (28 days and 29 days)\nmiles_28_days = calculate_miles(28)\nmiles_29_days = calculate_miles(29)\n\n# The fewest number of miles walked in February\nfewest_miles = min(miles_28_days, miles_29_days)\nprint(fewest_miles)\n```', "To determine the fewest number of miles Suzanne can walk in February, we need to consider the days on which she walks and the total number of days in February. February typically has 28 days in a non-leap year and 29 days in a leap year. We will assume a non-leap year for this calculation, but the same logic can be applied to a leap year.\n\nSuzanne walks every third day, so we need to find out how many days in February are multiples of 3. The days she walks are the 3rd, 6th, 9th, 12th, 15th, 18th, 21st, 24th, and 27th. This gives us a total of 9 days in February on which she walks.\n\nSince she walks 4 miles on each of these days, the total number of miles she walks in February is \\(9 \\times 4 = 36\\) miles.\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this calculation.\n```python\n# Number of days in February in a non-leap year\ndays_in_february = 28\n\n# Days on which Suzanne walks\nwalking_days = [day for day in range(1, days_in_february + 1) if day % 3 == 0]\n\n# Number of walking days\nnum_walking_days = len(walking_days)\n\n# Distance walked per day\ndistance_per_day = 4\n\n# Total distance walked in February\ntotal_distance = num_walking_days * distance_per_day\nprint(total_distance)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe fewest number of miles Suzanne can walk in February is \\(\\boxed{36}\\).", "To determine the fewest number of miles Suzanne can walk in February, we need to identify the days on which she walks. Since February has 28 days in a non-leap year, we need to find out how many days in February are every third day. The days on which Suzanne walks are the 3rd, 6th, 9th, 12th, 15th, 18th, 21st, 24th, and 27th. There are 9 such days in February.\n\nSince Suzanne walks 4 miles on each of these days, the total number of miles she walks in February is \\(9 \\times 4 = 36\\) miles.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Number of days in February\ndays_in_february = 28\n\n# Days on which Suzanne walks\nwalking_days = [day for day in range(1, days_in_february + 1) if day % 3 == 0]\n\n# Number of walking days in February\nnum_walking_days = len(walking_days)\n\n# Miles walked per day\nmiles_per_day = 4\n\n# Total miles walked in February\ntotal_miles = num_walking_days * miles_per_day\nprint(total_miles)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe fewest number of miles Suzanne can walk in February is \\(\\boxed{3