oes is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo count the number of factors of 5 in \\(42!\\), we can use the formula:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor\\) denotes the floor function, the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```python\n# Function to count the number of trailing zeroes in n!\ndef count_trailing_zeroes(n):\n    count = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\ntrailing_zeroes = count_trailing_zeroes(42)\nprint(trailing_zeroes)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", 'To determine the number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) (42 factorial), we need to count the number of times 10 is a factor in the product of integers from 1 to 42. Since \\(10 = 2 \\times 5\\), each pair of factors 2 and 5 will contribute to a trailing zero. \n\nHowever, in the factorial \\(42!\\), there are usually more factors of 2 than factors of 5. Therefore, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo count the number of factors of 5 in \\(42!\\):\n\n1. Count the multiples of 5.\n2. Count the multiples of \\(5^2 = 25\\), since each of these contributes an extra factor of 5.\n\nWe can implement this in Python to get the exact count.\n```python\ndef count_trailing_zeroes(n):\n    count = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\ntrailing_zeroes_42_factorial = count_trailing_zeroes(42)\nprint(trailing_zeroes_42_factorial)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).', "To determine the number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\), we need to find how many times 10 is a factor in the product of the integers from 1 to 42. Since \\(10 = 2 \\times 5\\), and in the factorial there are usually more multiples of 2 than 5, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo find this, we count the multiples of 5, 25, etc. (since \\(25 = 5^2\\), each multiple of 25 contributes an extra factor of 5).\n\nThe formula to find the number of trailing zeroes in \\(n!\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor\\) is the floor function, which rounds down to the nearest integer.\n\nFor \\(42!\\):\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{25} \\right\\rfloor = 8 + 1 = 9\n\\]\nsince \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{125} \\right\\rfloor = 0\\), and all higher terms will also be zero.\n\nSo, the number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is 9. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to calculate the number of trailing zeroes in n!\ndef trailing_zeroes(n):\n    count = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\nn = 42\nprint(trailing_zeroes(n))\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To determine the number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\), we need to count the number of times 10 is a factor in the product of integers from 1 to 42. Since \\(10 = 2 \\times 5\\), each pair of factors 2 and 5 will contribute to a trailing zero. \n\nHowever, in the factorial \\(42!\\), there are usually more factors of 2 than factors of 5. Therefore, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo count the number of factors of 5 in \\(42!\\):\n\n1. Count the multiples of 5.\n2. Count the multiples of \\(5^2 = 25\\), since each contributes an extra factor of 5.\n\nWe can use the following formula to count the number of factors of a prime \\(p\\) in \\(n!\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of factors of } p = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^3} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\nFor \\(42!\\) and \\(p = 5\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{25} \\right\\rfloor = 8 + 1 = 9\n\\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to confirm the result.\n```python\ndef count_factors_of_p_in_factorial(n, p):\n    count = 0\n    power = p\n    while power <= n:\n        count += n // power\n        power *= p\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\nn = 42\np = 5\ntrailing_zeroes = count_factors_of_p_in_factorial(n, p)\nprint(trailing_zeroes)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To determine the number of trailing zeros in \\(42!\\) (42 factorial), we need to find out how many times \\(42!\\) can be divided by 10. A trailing zero is produced by multiplying factors of 10, and each 10 is the product of the prime factors 2 and 5. Since there are usually more factors of 2 than factors of 5 in factorials, the number of trailing zeros is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo find the number of factors of 5 in \\(42!\\), we can use the formula:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{25} \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor\\) denotes the floor function, the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```pytho