, \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) can also be expressed using the determinant \\(D\\):\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| D \\right|.\n\\]\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\n6 = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| D \\right| \\implies \\left| D \\right| = 12.\n\\]\nThus, \\(D^2 = 12^2 = 144\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = sp.symbols('x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3')\n\n# Define the determinant\nD = sp.Matrix([[x1, y1, 1], [x2, y2, 1], [x3, y3, 1]]).det()\n\n# Given conditions\ncondition1 = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2 - 9\ncondition2 = (x1 - x3)**2 + (y1 - y3)**2 - 16\ncondition3 = (x2 - x3)**2 + (y2 - y3)**2 - 25\n\n# Solve the conditions to find the relationship between x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3\nsolutions = sp.solve([condition1, condition2, condition3], (x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3), dict=True)\n\n# Substitute the solutions into the determinant and find D^2\nD_squared = [D.subs(sol).evalf()**2 for sol in solutions]\nprint(D_squared)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that solving the system of equations symbolically is computationally intensive and may not be feasible within a reasonable time frame. Instead, we can use the geometric interpretation we derived earlier. We know that the points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) form a right triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5. The area of this triangle is 6, and the determinant \\(D\\) of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}\\) is related to the area by \\(D = \\pm 12\\). Therefore, \\(D^2 = 144\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{144}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to find the value of the determinant squared for the given matrix. Let's denote the determinant as \\(D\\). The determinant \\(D\\) of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}\\) can be written as:\n\\[ D = \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2). \\]\n\nGiven the equations:\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2 = 9,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_1 - x_3)^2 + (y_1 - y_3)^2 = 16,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_2 - x_3)^2 + (y_2 - y_3)^2 = 25,\n\\]\nwe can interpret these as distances between points \\((x_1, y_1)\\), \\((x_2, y_2)\\), and \\((x_3, y_3)\\) in the plane. Specifically, the points form a triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 5. This is a right triangle because \\(3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2\\).\n\nTo find \\(D^2\\), we can use the formula for the area of a triangle in terms of its vertices. The area \\(A\\) of the triangle can be calculated using the determinant formula:\n\\[ 2A = \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}. \\]\nSince the area of a right triangle with legs of lengths 3 and 4 is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6 \\), we have:\n\\[ 2A = 12. \\]\nThus,\n\\[ \\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = 12. \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ D^2 = 12^2 = 144. \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3 = sp.symbols('x1 y1 x2 y2 x3 y3')\n\n# Define the determinant\ndet = sp.Matrix([[x1, y1, 1], [x2, y2, 1], [x3, y3, 1]]).det()\n\n# Define the given equations\neq1 = (x1 - x2)**2 + (y1 - y2)**2 - 9\neq2 = (x1 - x3)**2 + (y1 - y3)**2 - 16\neq3 = (x2 - x3)**2 + (y2 - y3)**2 - 25\n\n# Solve the system of equations to find the coordinates of the points\nsolutions = sp.solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], (x1, y1, x2, y2, x3, y3), dict=True)\n\n# Calculate the determinant for each solution and find the squared value\ndet_squared = [det.subs(sol)**2 for sol in solutions]\n\n# Since we expect the same value for all solutions, we can just take the first one\ndet_squared_value = det_squared[0]\nprint(det_squared_value)\n