\) grid, there are \\(2 \\times (n-2)\\) possible \\(2 \\times 2\\) squares because each \\(2 \\times 2\\) square can be placed in any of the \\(n-2\\) positions along the length and any of the 2 positions along the width.\n\n3. **Counting the number of \\(3 \\times 3\\) squares:**\n   - For a \\(3 \\times n\\) grid, there are \\(1 \\times (n-3)\\) possible \\(3 \\times 3\\) squares because each \\(3 \\times 3\\) square can be placed in any of the \\(n-3\\) positions along the length and only 1 position along the width.\n\nThe total number of squares is the sum of these three quantities:\n\\[ 3(n-1) + 2(n-2) + 1(n-3) = 70 \\]\n\nLet's solve this equation for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[ 3(n-1) + 2(n-2) + (n-3) = 70 \\]\n\\[ 3n - 3 + 2n - 4 + n - 3 = 70 \\]\n\\[ 6n - 10 = 70 \\]\n\\[ 6n = 80 \\]\n\\[ n = \\frac{80}{6} = \\frac{40}{3} \\approx 13.33 \\]\n\nSince \\(n\\) must be an integer, we need to check if \\(n = 13\\) satisfies the equation. Let's verify by plugging \\(n = 13\\) back into the equation:\n\n\\[ 3(13-1) + 2(13-2) + (13-3) = 3(12) + 2(11) + 10 = 36 + 22 + 10 = 68 \\]\n\nThis is not equal to 70, so let's check \\(n = 14\\):\n\n\\[ 3(14-1) + 2(14-2) + (14-3) = 3(13) + 2(12) + 11 = 39 + 24 + 11 = 74 \\]\n\nThis is also not equal to 70. Since \\(n = 13\\) gives us 68 and \\(n = 14\\) gives us 74, the correct value of \\(n\\) must be between 13 and 14. Since \\(n\\) must be an integer, we need to check if there was an error in our assumptions or if we need to consider another approach.\n\nLet's use Python to verify the calculations and find the correct value of \\(n\\).\n```python\n# Function to calculate the total number of squares for a given n\ndef total_squares(n):\n    return 3*(n-1) + 2*(n-2) + (n-3)\n\n# Iterate to find the correct value of n\nfor n in range(1, 20):\n    if total_squares(n) == 70:\n        result = n\n        break\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'result' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the code execution.