ion\nexpression = c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Check the expression for specific values\nvalues = [\n    (1, 1, 1),  # a = b = c = 1\n    (2, 1, 1),  # a = 2, b = c = 1\n    (1, 2, 1),  # a = c = 1, b = 2\n    (1, 1, 2),  # a = b = 1, c = 2\n    (2, 2, 1),  # a = b = 2, c = 1\n    (1, 2, 2),  # a = 1, b = c = 2\n    (2, 1, 2)   # a = 2, b = 1, c = 2\n]\n\n# Evaluate the expression for the specific values\nresults = [(a_val, b_val, c_val, expression.subs({a: a_val, b: b_val, c: c_val})) for a_val, b_val, c_val in values]\nprint(results)\n```\n```output\n[(1, 1, 1, 5/2), (2, 1, 1, 11/6), (1, 2, 1, 11/6), (1, 1, 2, 11/6), (2, 2, 1, 13/12), (1, 2, 2, 13/12), (2, 1, 2, 13/12)]\n```\nThe output shows the values of the expression \\(\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}\\) for various specific values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. For \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), \\(c = 1\\), the value is \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\).\n2. For \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 1\\), \\(c = 1\\), the value is \\(\\frac{11}{6}\\).\n3. For \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 2\\), \\(c = 1\\), the value is \\(\\frac{11}{6}\\).\n4. For \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), \\(c = 2\\), the value is \\(\\frac{11}{6}\\).\n5. For \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 2\\), \\(c = 1\\), the value is \\(\\frac{13}{12}\\).\n6. For \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 2\\), \\(c = 2\\), the value is \\(\\frac{13}{12}\\).\n7. For \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 1\\), \\(c = 2\\), the value is \\(\\frac{13}{12}\\).\n\nFrom these results, we can see that the expression can take values less than \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\), but it does not seem to take any value less than 3. To confirm that the expression can take any value greater than or equal to 3, we need to consider the behavior of the expression as \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) vary.\n\nUsing the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}}.\n\\]\nThis shows that the expression is always greater than o