 cross product vector:\n\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 2^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = \\sqrt{49} = 7\n\\]\n\nNow we can use the formula for the magnitude of the cross product to find \\(\\sin(\\theta)\\):\n\n\\[\n7 = 2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin(\\theta) \\implies 7 = 14 \\sin(\\theta) \\implies \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) for which \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe principal value of \\(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) is \\(30^\\circ\\). Since we are asked for the smallest possible angle, we consider the principal value. Therefore, the smallest possible angle between \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is \\(30^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given values\nnorm_a = 2\nnorm_b = 7\ncross_product = np.array([3, 2, 6])\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\nmagnitude_cross_product = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\n\n# Calculate sin(theta)\nsin_theta = magnitude_cross_product / (norm_a * norm_b)\n\n# Calculate the angle in degrees\ntheta_radians = np.arcsin(sin_theta)\ntheta_degrees = np.degrees(theta_radians)\nprint(theta_degrees)\n```\n```output\n30.000000000000004\n```\nThe smallest possible angle between the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is \\(\\boxed{30}\\) degrees.", "To find the smallest possible angle between the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), we can use the properties of the cross product. The magnitude of the cross product of two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\| \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between the vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a}\\| = 2, \\quad \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| = 7, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 