e Expression:**\n   The expression is \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\). We know that \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x} = \\sec^2 x\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 x} = \\csc^2 x\\). So, the expression becomes \\(\\sec^2 10^\\circ + \\csc^2 20^\\circ + \\csc^2 40^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Use Trigonometric Identities:**\n   We can use the identity \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\) and \\(\\csc^2 x = 1 + \\cot^2 x\\). So, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ = 1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\csc^2 20^\\circ = 1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\csc^2 40^\\circ = 1 + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ + 1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + 1 + \\cot^2 40^\\circ = 3 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify Further:**\n   We need to find the values of \\(\\tan^2 10^\\circ\\), \\(\\cot^2 20^\\circ\\), and \\(\\cot^2 40^\\circ\\). We can use the fact that \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\), so \\(\\cot^2 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 20^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\cot^2 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 40^\\circ}\\). We can use the double-angle and triple-angle formulas to find these values.\n\n4. **Use Symmetry and Known Values:**\n   We can use the known values and symmetry properties of trigonometric functions. For example, we know that \\(\\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\) and \\(\\tan 60^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}\\). We can use these values to find the required trigonometric values.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to get the exact value.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangle1 = 10\nangle2 = 20\nangle3 = 40\n\n# Convert degrees to radians\nangle1_rad = sp.rad(angle1)\nangle2_rad = sp.rad(angle2)\nangle3_rad = sp.rad(angle3)\n\n# Calculate the trigonometric values\ntan10 = sp.tan(angle1_rad)\ncot20 = 1 / sp.tan(angle2_rad)\ncot40 = 1 / sp.tan(angle3_rad)\n\n# Calculate the expression\nexpression = 3 + tan10**2 + cot20**2 + cot40**2\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\ntan(pi/18)**2 + cot(pi/9)**2 + cot(2*pi/9)**2 + 3\n```\nThe output from the code is \\(\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)^2 + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 + \\cot\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 + 3\\). This is the exact form of the expression, but we need to simplify it further to get a numerical value.\n\nLet's manually simplify the expression using trigonometric identities and known values.\n\n1. **Rewrite the Expression:**\n   We have \\(\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right)^2 + \\cot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 + \\cot\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 + 3\\).\n\n2. **Use Known Values:**\n   We know that \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\), so \\(\\cot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)^2}\\) and \\(\\cot\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)^2}\\).\n\n3. **Simplify Further:**\n   We can use the fact that \\(\\tan(3x) = \\frac{3\\tan x - \\tan^3 x}{1 - 3\\tan^2 x}\\) to find the values of \\(\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)\\) and \\(\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right)\\).\n\nLet's use the known values and simplify the expression manually.\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{18}\\right) = \\tan(10^\\circ),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tan\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right) = \\tan(20^\\circ),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tan\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right) = \\tan(40^\\circ).\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\) and \\(\\csc^2 x = 1 + \\cot^2 x\\), we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\sec^2(10^\\circ) + \\csc^2(20^\\circ) + \\csc^2(40^\\circ) = 1 + \\tan^2(10^\\circ) + 1 + \\cot^2(20^\\circ) + 1 + \\cot^2(40^\\circ) = 3 + \\tan^2(10^\\circ) + \\cot^2(20^\\circ) + \\cot^2(40^\\circ).\n\\]\n\nWe can use the known values of \\(\\tan(10^\\circ)\\), \\(\\tan(20^\\circ)\\), and \