s.\n\n1. **Area Calculation of the First Parallelogram:**\n   The first parallelogram is bounded by the lines \\(y = ax + c\\), \\(y = ax + d\\), \\(y = bx + c\\), and \\(y = bx + d\\). The vertical distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = ax + d\\) is \\(|d - c|\\). The vertical distance between the lines \\(y = bx + c\\) and \\(y = bx + d\\) is also \\(|d - c|\\). The horizontal distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = bx + c\\) (or \\(y = ax + d\\) and \\(y = bx + d\\)) is the difference in the x-intercepts of these lines. The x-intercept of \\(y = ax + c\\) is \\(-\\frac{c}{a}\\) and the x-intercept of \\(y = bx + c\\) is \\(-\\frac{c}{b}\\). So, the horizontal distance is \\(\\left| -\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{c}{b} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{c(b - a)}{ab} \\right| = \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab}\\). The area of the parallelogram is the product of the vertical and horizontal distances, so:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = |d - c| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 18\n   \\]\n\n2. **Area Calculation of the Second Parallelogram:**\n   The second parallelogram is bounded by the lines \\(y = ax + c\\), \\(y = ax - d\\), \\(y = bx + c\\), and \\(y = bx - d\\). The vertical distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = ax - d\\) is \\(|c + d|\\). The vertical distance between the lines \\(y = bx + c\\) and \\(y = bx - d\\) is also \\(|c + d|\\). The horizontal distance between the lines \\(y = ax + c\\) and \\(y = bx + c\\) (or \\(y = ax - d\\) and \\(y = bx - d\\)) is the same as before, \\(\\frac{c|b - a|}{ab}\\). So, the area of the parallelogram is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = |c + d| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 72\n   \\]\n\n3. **Setting Up the Equations:**\n   From the first parallelogram, we have:\n   \\[\n   |d - c| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 18\n   \\]\n   From the second parallelogram, we have:\n   \\[\n   |c + d| \\cdot \\frac{c|b - a|}{ab} = 72\n   \\]\n   Dividing the second equation by the first equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|c + 