B = (-2 - 3, 1 - (-4), -1 - 1) = (-5, 5, -2)\n   \\]\n\n2. Compute the dot product of $\\vec{BA}$ and $\\vec{BC}$:\n   \\[\n   \\vec{BA} \\cdot \\vec{BC} = (-2) \\cdot (-5) + (-7) \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-2) = 10 - 35 - 2 = -27\n   \\]\n\n3. Compute the magnitudes of $\\vec{BA}$ and $\\vec{BC}$:\n   \\[\n   \\|\\vec{BA}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-7)^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 49 + 1} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\|\\vec{BC}\\| = \\sqrt{(-5)^2 + 5^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 25 + 4} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. Use the dot product formula to find the cosine of the angle:\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\vec{BA} \\cdot \\vec{BC}}{\\|\\vec{BA}\\| \\|\\vec{BC}\\|} = \\frac{-27}{3\\sqrt{6} \\cdot 3\\sqrt{6}} = \\frac{-27}{54} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. Find the angle $\\theta$ in radians:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\arccos\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\text{ radians}\n   \\]\n\n6. Convert the angle to degrees:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\times \\frac{180}{\\pi} = 120 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the points A, B, and C\nA = np.array([1, -11, 2])\nB = np.array([3, -4, 1])\nC = np.array([-2, 1, -1])\n\n# Compute the vectors BA and BC\nBA = A - B\nBC = C - B\n\n# Compute the dot product of BA and BC\ndot_product = np.dot(BA, BC)\n\n# Compute the magnitudes of BA and BC\nmagnitude_BA = np.linalg.norm(BA)\nmagnitude_BC = np.linalg.norm(BC)\n\n# Compute the cosine of the angle\ncos_theta = dot_product / (magnitude_BA * magnitude_BC)\n\n# Compute the angle in radians\ntheta_radians = np.arccos(cos_theta)\n\n# Convert the angle to degrees\ntheta_degrees = np.degrees(theta_radians)\nprint(theta_degrees)\n```\n```output\n120.00000000000001\n```\nThe angle $\\angle ABC$ is $\\boxed{120}$ degrees.", "To find the angle $\\angle ABC$ formed by the points $A = (1, -11, 2)$, $B = (3, -4, 1)$, and $C = (-2, 1, -1)$, we can use the dot product formula for vectors.