are perpendicular to the tangents \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\), respectively. This means \\(\\angle JOH = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle JNH = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - The quadrilateral \\(JONH\\) is formed by the radii \\(JO\\) and \\(JN\\) and the tangents \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\). The sum of the interior angles of any quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the Sum of the Angles:**\n   - The angles \\(\\angle JOH\\) and \\(\\angle JNH\\) are each \\(90^\\circ\\). So, the sum of these two angles is \\(90^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ\\).\n   - The remaining angles in the quadrilateral are \\(\\angle J\\) and \\(\\angle H\\). Since the sum of the angles in the quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), we have:\n     \\[\n     \\angle J + \\angle H + 180^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying this, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\angle J + \\angle H = 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of degrees in the sum of \\(m\\angle J\\) and \\(m\\angle H\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code using sympy to ensure the result is accurate.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles\nangle_J = sp.Symbol('angle_J')\nangle_H = sp.Symbol('angle_H')\n\n# Since angle JOH and angle JNH are each 90 degrees, their sum is 180 degrees\nangle_JOH = 90\nangle_JNH = 90\nsum_of_tangent_angles = angle_JOH + angle_JNH\n\n# The sum of the angles in the quadrilateral JONH is 360 degrees\n# So, angle_J + angle_H + sum_of_tangent_angles = 360\nequation = sp.Eq(angle_J + angle_H + sum_of_tangent_angles, 360)\n\n# Solve for the sum of angle_J and angle_H\nsum_of_angles_J_and_H = sp.solve(equation, angle_J + angle_H)[0]\nprint(sum_of_angles_J_and_H)\n```\n```output\n180\n```\nThe sum of \\(m\\angle J\\) and \\(m\\angle H\\) is \\(\\boxed{180}\\) degrees.", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric properties of the given circle and the tangent lines.\n\n1. **Identify Key Properties**:\n   - Since \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\)