oot of the number\nif not is_perfect_square:\n    simplest_radical_form = sp.sqrt(number)\nelse:\n    simplest_radical_form = sp.sqrt(number)  # In case it is a perfect square, but it won't be here\n\nprint(simplest_radical_form)\n```\n```output\nsqrt(53)\n```\nThe output confirms that $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form because 53 is a prime number and cannot be factored into any perfect squares other than 1.\n\nTherefore, the simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$ is $\\boxed{\\sqrt{53}}$.", "To express $\\sqrt{53}$ in simplest radical form, we need to check if 53 can be factored into a perfect square and another integer. If it can, we can simplify the radical. Otherwise, $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest form.\n\nLet's check the factors of 53. The factors of 53 are 1 and 53, which means 53 is a prime number. Since 53 is a prime number, it cannot be factored into a perfect square and another integer. Therefore, $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form.\n\nThe simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$ is $\\sqrt{53}$.\n\nHere is the Python code to confirm this:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the number\nnumber = 53\n\n# Check if the number is a perfect square\nis_perfect_square = sp.is_square(number)\n\n# If it is a perfect square, simplify the radical\nif is_perfect_square:\n    simplified_radical = sp.sqrt(number)\nelse:\n    simplified_radical = sp.sqrt(number)\n\nprint(simplified_radical)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: module 'sympy' has no attribute 'is_square'\n```\nIt seems there was an error because the `sympy` module does not have an `is_square` attribute. Instead, we can check if a number is a perfect square by taking its integer square root and squaring it back to see if we get the original number.\n\nHere is the corrected Python code to confirm that $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the number\nnumber = 53\n\n# Check if the number is a perfect 