ymbolic computation are approximately \\(0.171572875253810\\) and \\(5.82842712474619\\). These values correspond to \\(3 - 2\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\\) respectively.\n\nSo the solutions to the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3 - 2\\sqrt{2}, 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]", "To solve the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\), we can use the following trigonometric identities and properties:\n\n1. \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}(x) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for \\(x > 0\\).\n2. \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nGiven the equation, we can simplify the argument of the sine function using the identity above. For \\(x \\neq 0\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n\\]\nThus, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{1}{3},\n\\]\nwhich is not true. This means there are no solutions for \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nNow, let's consider the case when \\(x = 0\\). The expression \\(\\tan^{-1}(0) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right)\\) is undefined because \\(\\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right)\\) is undefined. Therefore, \\(x = 0\\) is not a valid solution either.\n\nSince we have checked all possible cases and found no valid solutions, the final answer is that there are no solutions.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No solution}}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function to check the equation\ndef check_equation(x):\n    if x == 0:\n        return False\n    angle = np.arctan(x) + np.arctan(1/x)\n    return np.isclose(np.sin(angle), 1/3)\n\n# Test a range of values for x\ntest_values = np.linspace(-10,