h) simultaneously. To do this, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the number of rotations per minute of both gears.\n\n1. First, we convert the rotation speed of the first gear from a mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\\[ 33\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{100}{3} \\]\nSo, the first gear rotates $\\frac{100}{3}$ times per minute, and the second gear rotates 45 times per minute.\n\n2. To find the time it takes for both gears to be in the same position again, we need to find the LCM of $\\frac{100}{3}$ and 45. Since the LCM of two fractions $\\frac{a}{b}$ and $c$ is the LCM of $a$ and $c$ divided by the greatest common divisor (GCD) of $b$ and 1 (which is 1 in this case), we can find the LCM of 100 and 45 and then divide by 3.\n\n3. The LCM of 100 and 45 is the smallest number that is a multiple of both 100 and 45. We can find the LCM using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(a, b) = \\frac{|a \\cdot b|}{\\text{GCD}(a, b)} \\]\nwhere GCD is the greatest common divisor.\n\n4. The GCD of 100 and 45 is 5, so:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(100, 45) = \\frac{100 \\cdot 45}{5} = 900 \\]\nSince the first gear rotates $\\frac{100}{3}$ times per minute, it will take $\\frac{900}{100} \\cdot 3 = 27$ minutes for the first gear to complete 900 rotations. Since the second gear rotates 45 times per minute, it will also take 20 minutes to complete 900 rotations. Therefore, the time it takes for both gears to be in the same position again is the LCM of 27 and 20 minutes.\n\n5. The LCM of 27 and 20 is the smallest number that is a multiple of both 27 and 20. Since 27 and 20 have no common factors other than 1, their LCM is simply their product:\n\\[ \\text{LCM}(27, 20) = 27 \\cdot 20 = 540 \\text{ minutes} \\]\nSince there are 60 seconds in a minute, the time in seconds is:\n\\[ 540 \\cdot 60 = 32400 \\text{ seconds} \\]\n\nHowever, we need to check if there is a smaller time that satisfies the condition. Since the gears are rotating at different speeds, we need to find the smallest time in seconds when both gears are back at their initial positions. This can be found by checking the time in seconds when both gears complete an integer number of rotations.\n\nLet's use Python to find the exact time in seconds.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rotation speeds\ngear1_speed = 100 / 3  # rotations per minute\ngear2_speed = 45  # rotations per minute\n\n# Convert speeds to rotations per second\ngear1_speed_per_sec = gear1_speed / 60\ngear2_speed_per_sec = gear2_speed / 60\n\n# Function to find the time in seconds when both gears are back at their initial position\ndef find_time_for_initial_position(gear1_speed_per_sec, gear2_speed_per_sec):\n    # Find the LCM of the two speeds\n    lcm_speeds = (gear1_speed_per_sec * gear2_speed_per_sec) / math.gcd(gear1_speed_per_sec, gear2_speed_per_sec)\n    # Convert the LCM speed to time in seconds\n    time_in_seconds = lcm_speeds * 60\n    return time_in_seconds\n\n# Calculate the time in seconds\ntime_in_seconds = find_time_for_initial_position(gear1_speed_per_sec, gear2_speed_per_sec)\nprint(time_in_seconds)\n```\n```output\nTypeError: 'float' object cannot be interpreted as an integer\n```\nThe error occurs because the `math.gcd` function in Python expects integer inputs, but we are providing it with floating-point numbers. To fix this, we can convert the rotation speeds to integers by multiplying by 60 (since we are working in seconds) and then finding the GCD of the resulting integers.\n\nLet's adjust the code accordingly.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rotation speeds in rotations per second\ngear1_speed_per_sec = 100 / 3 / 60\ngear2_speed_per_sec = 45 / 60\n\n# Convert speeds to integers by multiplying by 60\ngear1_speed_int = int(gear1_speed_per_sec * 60)\ngear2_speed_int = int(gear2_speed_per_sec * 60)\n\n# Function to find the time in seconds when both gears are back at their initial position\ndef find_time_for_initial_position(gear1_speed_int, gear2_speed_int):\n    # Find the LCM of the two speeds\n    lcm_speeds