{1}{2} \\times (AD + BC) \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h = 42\n\\]\nThis gives us the equation:\n\\[\n(a + b) \\times h = 84\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). The area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times CD \\times AC = \\frac{1}{2} \\times c \\times h\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAD\\), and \\(\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that the height \\(h\\) is the same for both \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(\\triangle ABC\\). The area of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n[\\triangle ABC] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BC \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h\n\\]\n\nThe area of \\(\\triangle ABD\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n[\\triangle ABD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AD \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times a \\times h\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AD} \\parallel \\overline{BC}\\), the height \\(h\\) is the same for both \\(\\triangle ABD\\) and \\(\\triangle ABC\\), and the sum of the areas of \\(\\triangle ABD\\) and \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is equal to the area of the trapezoid:\n\\[\n[\\triangle ABD] + [\\triangle ABC] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times a \\times h + \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h = 42\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAD\\), the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is half of the area of the trapezoid minus the area of \\(\\triangle ABC\\):\n\\[\n[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times c \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h - \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h = 42 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}\\), the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times c \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (a + b) \\times h - \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h = 42 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAD\\), the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n\\[\n[\\triangle 