ring when \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\) becomes less than 1, which happens at \\( x = 0 \\). For \\( x > 0 \\), the values of \\( f(x) \\) will continue to decrease, but they will never reach a value greater than 1 or less than -1 because \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\) will never actually reach 0.\n\nTherefore, the distinct values in the range of \\( f(x) \\) are 1, 0, and all negative integers. The number of distinct values is infinite because there are infinitely many negative integers.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\infty}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code snippet to verify the behavior of the function for a range of \\( x \\) values.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function f(x)\ndef f(x):\n    return np.floor((-5/8)**x)\n\n# Generate a range of x values from 0 to a large number\nx_values = np.linspace(0, 100, 10000)\nf_values = f(x_values)\n\n# Get the unique values in the range of f(x)\nunique_f_values = np.unique(f_values)\nprint((unique_f_values, len(unique_f_values)))\n```", 'To determine how many distinct values exist in the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( x \\in [0, \\infty) \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function.\n\n1. **Behavior of \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\)**:\n    - For \\( x = 0 \\), \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^0 = 1\\), so \\( f(0) = \\left\\lfloor 1 \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\).\n    - For \\( x > 0 \\), \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\) is not a real number for non-integer values of \\( x \\) because it involves raising a negative number to a non-integer power, which results in a complex number. However, for integer values of \\( x \\), \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\) is a real number.\n    - For integer values of \\( x \\), \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x\\) alternates between positive and negative values. Specifically:\n        - For even \\( x \\), \\(\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)^x = \\left(\