expression \\(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\\), we can break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's simplify the individual components inside the expression:\n\n1. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}\\):\n\\[\n\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} = \\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5}} = \\dfrac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{4 \\cdot 5} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n\\]\n\n2. Simplify \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{845} = \\sqrt{169 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{169} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = 13\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} = \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9}\n\\]\n\n3. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{45}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can substitute these simplified components into the original expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\n\\]\n\nCombine the terms in the numerator:\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{5} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{13}{9} + 3 \\right)\n\\]\nTo add the fractions, we need a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{9}{36}, \\quad \\dfrac{13}{9} = \\dfrac{52}{36}, \\quad 3 = \\dfrac{108}{36}\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{13}{9} + 3 = \\dfrac{9 + 52 + 108}{36} = \\dfrac{169}{36}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{5} \\left( \\dfrac{169}{36} \\right) = \\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute this back into the original expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}}{\\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169}{36}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{169}}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n\\]\n\nSo, the simplified fraction is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{13}{6}}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nnumerator = (5 / sp.sqrt(80)) + (sp.sqrt(845) / 9) + sp.sqrt(45)\r\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\r\nexpression = sp.sqrt(numerator / denominator)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```", "To solve the given expression, we'll break it down step by step and use Python to ensure accuracy. The expression we need to simplify is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify the expression inside the square root step by step.\n\n1. Simplify \\(\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5}} = \\frac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{4 \\cdot 5} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n\\]\n\n2. Simplify \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{169 \\cdot 5}}{9} = \\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9}\n\\]\n\n3. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{45}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these simplified expressions back into the original expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]\n\nCombine the terms in the numerator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{5} \\left( \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{13}{9} + 3 \\right)\n\\]\n\nTo add the fractions, find a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{9}{36}, \\quad \\frac{13}{9} = \\frac{52}{36}, \\quad 3 = \\frac{108}{36}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{13}{9} + 3 = \\frac{9 + 52 + 108}{36} = \\frac{169}{36}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\frac{169}{36}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{169}{36}\n\\]\n\nNow we take the square root of this result:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{169}{36}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{169}}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\frac{13}{6}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{13}{6}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Define the components of the expression\r\nnumerator = (5 / math.sqrt(80)) + (math.sqrt(845) / 9) + math.sqrt(45)\r\ndenominator = math.sqrt(5)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the expression\r\nexpression_value = math.sqrt(numerator / denominator)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression to a fraction\r\nfrom fractions import Fraction\r\nsimplified_fraction = Fraction(expression_value).limit_denominator()\r\n\r\nprint((expression_value, simplified_fraction))\n```", "To simplify the given expression \\(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\\), we need to break it down step by step.\n\n1. Simplify the terms inside the numerator:\n   - Simplify \\(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} = \\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5}} = \\dfrac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{5}}{20} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{845} = \\sqrt{169 \\cdot 5} = 13\\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n     So,\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} = \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify \\(\\sqrt{45}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n   - Combine all the simplified terms in the numerator:\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5} = \\dfrac{9\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{52\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{108\\sqrt{5}}{36} = \\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}\n     \\]\n2. Now, substitute this back into the original expression:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}}{\\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36\\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169}{36}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{169}}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n   \\]\n3. The final answer is \\(\\dfrac{13}{6}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression\nnumerator = sp.Rational(5, sp.sqrt(80)) + sp.sqrt(845) / 9 + sp.sqrt(45)\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\nexpression = sp.sqrt(numerator / denominator)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\nTypeError: invalid input: 4*sqrt(5)\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the way the expression was defined. Let's correct it and simplify the expression step by step using SymPy.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the components of the expression\nsqrt_80 = sp.sqrt(80)\nsqrt_845 = sp.sqrt(845)\nsqrt_45 = sp.sqrt(45)\nsqrt_5 = sp.sqrt(5)\n\n# Simplify each component\nterm1 = 5 / sqrt_80\nterm2 = sqrt_845 / 9\nterm3 = sqrt_45\n\n# Combine the terms in the numerator\nnumerator = term1 + term2 + term3\n\n# Define the full expression\nexpression = sp.sqrt(numerator / sqrt_5)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n13/6\n```\nThe simplified expression is \\(\\frac{13}{6}\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{13}{6}}\\).\n\nWhat is the sum of the squares of the lengths of the $\\textbf{medians}$ of a triangle whose side lengths are $10,$ $10,$ and $12$?\nTo find the sum of the squares of the lengths of the medians of a triangle with side lengths 10, 10, and 12, we can use the formula for the length of a median in a triangle. For a triangle with sides $a$, $b$, and $c$, the length of the median to the side $c$ (denoted as $m_c$) is given by:\n\\[ m_c = \\sqrt{\\frac{2a^2 + 2b^2 - c^2}{4}}. \\]\n\nIn this triangle, the sides are $a = 10$, $b = 10$, and $c = 12$. We need to find the lengths of the medians to each of these sides and then compute the sum of their squares.\n\n1. The median to the side of length 12 (denoted as $m_{12}$):\n\\[ m_{12} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(10^2) + 2(10^2) - 12^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(100) + 2(100) - 144}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{200 + 200 - 144}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{256}{4}} = \\sqrt{64} = 8. \\]\n\n2. The median to one of the sides of length 10 (denoted as $m_{10}$):\n\\[ m_{10} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(10^2) + 2(12^2) - 10^2}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(100) + 2(144) - 100}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{200 + 288 - 100}{4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{388}{4}} = \\sqrt{97}. \\]\n\nSince the triangle is isosceles, the other median to the other side of length 10 will also have the same length, i.e., $\\sqrt{97}$.\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the squares of these medians:\n\\[ m_{12}^2 + m_{10}^2 + m_{10}^2 = 8^2 + (\\sqrt{97})^2 + (\\sqrt{97})^2 = 64 + 97 + 97 = 258. \\]\n\nSo, the sum of the squares of the lengths of the medians is $258$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Side lengths of the triangle\na = 10\nb = 10\nc = 12\n\n# Length of the median to the side of length 12\nm_c = math.sqrt((2*a**2 + 2*b**2 - c**2) / 4)\n\n# Length of the median to one of the sides of length 10\nm_a = math.sqrt((2*b**2 + 2*c**2 - a**2) / 4)\n\n# Since the triangle is isosceles, the other median to the other side of length 10 will have the same length\nm_b = m_a\n\n# Sum of the squares of the medians\nsum_of_squares = m_c**2 + m_a**2 + m_b**2\nprint(sum_of_squares)\n```\n```output\n258.0\n```\nThe sum of the squares of the lengths of the medians of the triangle with side lengths 10, 10, and 12 is \\(\\boxed{258}\\).\n\nFind the number of ordered triples of positive integers  $(a, b, c)$ , where  $a, b,c$  is a strictly increasing arithmetic progression,  $a + b + c = 2019$ , and there is a triangle with side lengths  $a, b$ , and  $c$ .\nTo solve the problem, we need to find all ordered triples \\((a, b, c)\\) where \\(a, b, c\\) form a strictly increasing arithmetic progression, \\(a + b + c = 2019\\), and \\(a, b, c\\) can be the side lengths of a triangle. Let's break it down into manageable steps:\n\n1. **Arithmetic Progression Condition:**\n   Since \\(a, b, c\\) form an arithmetic progression, we can write \\(b = a + d\\) and \\(c = a + 2d\\) for some positive integer \\(d\\). Therefore, the equation \\(a + b + c = 2019\\) becomes:\n   \\[\n   a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) = 2019 \\implies 3a + 3d = 2019 \\implies a + d = 673.\n   \\]\n   Hence, \\(d = 673 - a\\).\n\n2. **Triangle Inequality Conditions:**\n   For \\(a, b, c\\) to be the side lengths of a triangle, they must satisfy the triangle inequalities:\n   \\[\n   a + b > c, \\quad a + c > b, \\quad b + c > a.\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(b = a + d\\) and \\(c = a + 2d\\) into these inequalities, we get:\n   \\[\n   a + (a + d) > (a + 2d) \\implies 2a + d > a + 2d \\implies a > d,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + (a + 2d) > (a + d) \\implies 2a + 2d > a + d \\implies a + d > 0 \\quad (\\text{always true since } a, d > 0),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (a + d) + (a + 2d) > a \\implies 2a + 3d > a \\implies a + 3d > 0 \\quad (\\text{always true since } a, d > 0).\n   \\]\n   The only relevant inequality is \\(a > d\\). Since \\(d = 673 - a\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   a > 673 - a \\implies 2a > 673 \\implies a > \\frac{673}{2} \\implies a \\geq 337.\n   \\]\n   Also, since \\(a < 673\\) (because \\(d > 0\\)), we have \\(337 \\leq a < 673\\).\n\n3. **Counting the Valid Triples:**\n   The number of valid values for \\(a\\) is the number of integers from 337 to 672 inclusive, which is \\(672 - 337 + 1 = 336\\).\n\nSo, the number of ordered triples \\((a, b, c)\\) is \\(\\boxed{336}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# We need to count the number of valid triples (a, b, c) where a, b, c form an arithmetic progression,\n# a + b + c = 2019, and a, b, c can be the side lengths of a triangle.\n\ncount = 0\nfor a in range(337, 673):\n    d = 673 - a\n    b = a + d\n    c = a + 2 * d\n    if a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a:\n        count += 1\n\nprint(count)\n```\n\nGiven the sets $M=\\{x|x^2=1\\}$ and $N=\\{x|ax=1\\}$, if $N \\subseteq M$, then the set of values for the real number $a$ is ( ).\n\nA: $\\{1\\}$\n\nB: $\\{-1,1\\}$\n\nC: $\\{1,0\\}$\n\nD: $\\{1,-1,0\\}$\nTo solve this problem, we need to understand the sets \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and the condition \\(N \\subseteq M\\).\n\n1. **Set \\(M\\)**: The set \\(M\\) is defined by the equation \\(x^2 = 1\\). Solving this equation, we get \\(x = 1\\) or \\(x = -1\\). So, \\(M = \\{1, -1\\}\\).\n\n2. **Set \\(N\\)**: The set \\(N\\) is defined by the equation \\(ax = 1\\). If \\(a = 0\\), then the equation \\(ax = 1\\) has no solution, so \\(N = \\emptyset\\). For \\(a \\neq 0\\), the solution to the equation \\(ax = 1\\) is \\(x = \\frac{1}{a}\\). So, \\(N = \\left\\{\\frac{1}{a}\\right\\}\\).\n\n3. **Condition \\(N \\subseteq M\\)**: This means that every element of \\(N\\) must be an element of \\(M\\). So, if \\(N \\neq \\emptyset\\), then \\(\\frac{1}{a} \\in M\\). Since \\(M = \\{1, -1\\}\\), \\(\\frac{1}{a}\\) must be either 1 or -1. This gives us the equations \\(\\frac{1}{a} = 1\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{a}", "To solve the given problem, we need to simplify the expression step by step. The expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\n\\]\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1. Simplify the numerator:\n   - Simplify \\(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} = \\dfrac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{4 \\cdot 5} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{845} = \\sqrt{169 \\cdot 5} = 13\\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n     So,\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} = \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify \\(\\sqrt{45}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n   - Combine the simplified terms in the numerator:\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5}\n     \\]\n     Find a common denominator (36) to combine the fractions:\n     \\[\n     \\dfrac{9\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{52\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{108\\sqrt{5}}{36} = \\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}\n     \\]\n\n2. Substitute the simplified numerator into the original expression:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}}{\\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{169}{36}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{169}}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the simplified fraction is \\(\\dfrac{13}{6}\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nnumerator = (5 / sp.sqrt(80)) + (sp.sqrt(845) / 9) + sp.sqrt(45)\r\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\r\nexpression = sp.sqrt(numerator / denominator)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n13/6\n```\nThe simplified fraction of the given expression is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{13}{6}}\\).", "To solve the given expression, we need to simplify it step by step. The expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} + \\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\n\\]\n\nLet's break it down into manageable parts:\n\n1. Simplify the individual terms in the numerator.\n2. Combine the simplified terms.\n3. Divide by \\(\\sqrt{5}\\).\n4. Take the square root of the resulting fraction.\n\nWe'll use Python and sympy to perform these steps.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression components\nnumerator = (5 / sp.sqrt(80)) + (sp.sqrt(845) / 9) + sp.sqrt(45)\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\nfraction = numerator / denominator\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nsimplified_fraction = sp.simplify(fraction)\n\n# Take the square root of the simplified fraction\nfinal_result = sp.sqrt(simplified_fraction)\n\nprint(final_result)\n```\n```output\nsqrt(130)/9\n```\nThe simplified form of the given expression \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} + \\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{130}}{9}}\\).", "Let's break down the problem step-by-step and solve it using Python and SymPy.\n\n1. **Simplify the numerator:**\n   - Simplify each term in the numerator separately.\n   - Combine the simplified terms.\n\n2. **Simplify the entire fraction inside the square root.**\n\n3. **Take the square root