the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can first generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and observe if it exhibits any periodicity. If it does, we can use this periodicity to find the 100th term modulo 4 without having to compute the entire 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence.\n\nLet's generate the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence modulo 4 repeats every 6 terms: \\(1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0\\). Therefore, the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 will be the same as the 4th term of the sequence modulo 4 (since \\(100 \\mod 6 = 4\\)).\n\nSo, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is the same as the remainder when the 4th term is divided by 4, which is 3.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to generate Fibonacci sequence modulo m and find the nth term\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```", 'To determine the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can use the property of the Fibonacci sequence known as "Pisano periods." The sequence of Fibonacci numbers modulo a given integer (in this case, 4) eventually becomes periodic. We need to find the Pisano period for modulo 4 and then use it to determine the 100th term modulo 4.\n\nFirst, let\'s compute the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 to identify the period:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. So the Pisano period for modulo 4 is 6. Therefore, the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term (since $100 \\mod 6 = 4$) of the sequence modulo 4. From the sequence above, we see that $F_4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$. Thus, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet\'s confirm this by writing a Python function to compute the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and verify the result.\n```python\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, mod):\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for i in range(3, n+1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % mod\n    return b\n\n# Compute the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4\nfib_100_mod_4 = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(fib_100_mod_4)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).', 'The expression \\(513^2 - 487^2\\) can be simplified using the difference of squares formula, which states that \\(a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b)\\). Here, \\(a = 513\\) and \\(b = 487\\). So we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n513^2 - 487^2 = (513 - 487)(513 + 487)\n\\]\nNow we can calculate the values inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n513 - 487 = 26\n\