ep:\n\n1. Start with the inequality:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n2. Multiply all parts of the inequality by 7:\n   \\[\n   7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} < x < 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the fractions, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{7}{4} < x < \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n3. Convert the fractions to decimal form to better understand the range:\n   \\[\n   1.75 < x < 2.3333\\ldots\n   \\]\n4. The only integer that falls within this range is \\( x = 2 \\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the inequality boundaries\nlower_bound = 7 / 4\nupper_bound = 7 / 3\n\n# Find the integer within the range (lower_bound, upper_bound)\ninteger_in_range = [x for x in range(int(lower_bound) + 1, int(upper_bound) + 1)]\n\nprint(integer_in_range)\n```\n```output\n[2]\n```\nThe integer \\( x \\) that satisfies the inequality \\(\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}$, we can break it into two separate inequalities and solve for $x$:\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7}$\n2. $\\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nFirst, we solve $\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{x}{7}$ by multiplying both sides by 7:\n\\[ \\frac{7}{4} < x \\]\nSince $x$ is an integer, we take the ceiling of $\\frac{7}{4}$, which is 2. So, $x > 2$.\n\nNext, we solve $\\frac{x}{7} < \\frac{1}{3}$ by multiplying both sides by 7:\n\\[ x < \\frac{7}{3} \\]\nSince $x$ is an integer, we take the floor of $\\frac{7}{3}$, which is 2. So, $x < 3$.\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\\[ 2 < x < 3 \\]\nThe only integer that satisfies this inequality is $x = 2$. However, since $x$ must be strictly greater than 2 and strictly less than 3, there is no integer that satisfies the inequality. Therefore, we need to check the possible values around these bounds.\n\nLet's use Python to verify this step-by-step.\n```python\n# Define the bounds\nlower_bound = 7 / 4\nupper_bound = 7 