at the left-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) from below is equal to the right-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) from above, and both are equal to the value of the function at \\(n = a\\).\n\nThe function is defined as:\n\\[f(n) =\n\\begin{cases}\n4n+3 & \\text{if } n < a, \\\\\n7n-12 & \\text{if } n \\geq a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor the function to be continuous at \\(n = a\\), the following must hold:\n\\[\n\\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = f(a).\n\\]\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) from below is:\n\\[\n\\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = 4a + 3.\n\\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) from above is:\n\\[\n\\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = 7a - 12.\n\\]\n\nSince the function is continuous at \\(n = a\\), we have:\n\\[\n4a + 3 = 7a - 12.\n\\]\n\nNow we solve this equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[\n4a + 3 = 7a - 12.\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to isolate \\(a\\):\n\\[\n3 + 12 = 7a - 4a,\n\\]\n\\[\n15 = 3a,\n\\]\n\\[\na = 5.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by substituting \\(a = 5\\) back into the function to ensure the left-hand limit and right-hand limit are equal.\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches 5 from below is:\n\\[\n4(5) + 3 = 20 + 3 = 23.\n\\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches 5 from above is:\n\\[\n7(5) - 12 = 35 - 12 = 23.\n\\]\n\nSince both limits are equal to 23, the function is indeed continuous at \\(n = 5\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To find the value of \\( t(g(16)) \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\( g(16) \\) using the function \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{x} \\).\n2. Substitute the result from step 1 into the function \\( t(x) = 3 - g(x) \\).\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1. Calculate \\( g(16) \\):\n\\[ g(16) = \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( g(16) \\) into \\( t(x) \\):\n\\[ t(g(16)) = t(4) = 3 - g(4) \\]\nSince \\( g(4) = \\sqrt{4} = 2 \\),\n\\[ t(4) = 3 - 2 = 1 \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is \\( t(g(16)) = 1 \\).\n\nLet's 