\\) and \\(y\\) are chosen from the interval \\((0,3)\\) is a square with side length 3, so its area is \\(3 \\times 3 = 9\\).\n\nThe region that satisfies the inequalities \\(1 + x > y\\), \\(1 + y > x\\), and \\(x + y > 1\\) is a bit more complex. We can visualize this region in the \\(xy\\)-plane. The inequalities \\(1 + x > y\\) and \\(1 + y > x\\) define two regions that intersect with the region \\(x + y > 1\\). The intersection of these regions forms a hexagon within the square.\n\nTo find the area of this hexagon, we can subtract the area of the two right triangles that are outside the hexagon but within the square. Each of these triangles has legs of length 1, so their area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\). There are two such triangles, so their total area is \\(2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 1\\). The area of the hexagon is then \\(9 - 1 = 8\\).\n\nSo, the probability that a triangle with sides of length 1, \\(x\\), and \\(y\\) exists is the area of the hexagon divided by the area of the square, which is \\(\\frac{8}{9}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# The area of the square is 3 * 3 = 9\narea_square = 3 * 3\n\n# The area of the two right triangles that are outside the hexagon but within the square is 2 * (1/2 * 1 * 1) = 1\narea_triangles = 2 * (1/2 * 1 * 1)\n\n# The area of the hexagon is the area of the square minus the area of the two triangles\narea_hexagon = area_square - area_triangles\n\n# The probability is the area of the hexagon divided by the area of the square\nprobability = area_hexagon / area_square\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n0.8888888888888888\n```\nThe probability that a triangle with sides of length 1, \\(x\\), and \\(y\\) exists when \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are selected at random from the interval \\((0,3)\\) is \\(\\frac{8}{9}\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{9}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the conditions under which three lengths can form a