e function \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[ f(x) = \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\sin \\left(2x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\cos 2x + a. \\]\nUsing the sum-to-product identities, we can simplify the sum of sines:\n\\[ \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\sin \\left(2x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 2 \\sin 2x \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} = 2 \\sin 2x \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\sqrt{3} \\sin 2x. \\]\nSo, the function simplifies to:\n\\[ f(x) = \\sqrt{3} \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x + a. \\]\nWe can further simplify this using the identity \\( \\sqrt{3} \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x = 2 \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\):\n\\[ f(x) = 2 \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + a. \\]\nNow, let\'s find the derivative of \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[ f\'(x) = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cos \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 4 \\cos \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right). \\]\nThe function \\(f(x)\\) is monotonically decreasing when \\(f\'(x) < 0\\), i.e., when \\(\\cos \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) < 0\\). The cosine function is negative in the intervals \\((\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi, \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi)\\) for any integer \\(k\\). So, for our function:\n\\[ \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi < 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6} < \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi. \\]\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\\[ \\frac{\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6} < 2x < \\frac{3\\pi}{2} + 2k\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{3\\pi - \\pi}{6} + 2k\\pi < 2x < \\frac{9\\pi - \\pi}{6} + 2k\\pi, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{\\pi}{3} + k\\pi < x < \\frac{2\\pi}{3} + k\\pi. \\]\nSince we are looking for the intervals within one period \\([0, \\pi)\\), the intervals where the function is monotonically decreasing are:\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right). \\]\n\n### Part III: Find the value of \\(a\\) if the minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(-2\\) for \\(x \\in \\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\\)\nThe function \\(f(x) = 2 \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + a\\) has a minimum value when \\(\\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = -1\\). So, the minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) is:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot (-1) + a = -2 + a. \\]\nGiven that the minimum value is \\(-2\\), we have:\n\\[ -2 + a = -2, \\]\n\\[ a = 0. \\]\nLet\'s confirm this by implementing the steps in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and function\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\nf = sp.sin(2*x + sp.pi/6) + sp.sin(2*x - sp.pi/6) + sp.cos(2*x) + sp.symbols(\'a\')\n\n# Simplify the function\nf_simplified = sp.simplify(f)\nf_simplified = sp.simplify(f_simplified.rewrite(sp.sin))\n\n# Derivative of the function\nf_prime = sp.diff(f_simplified, x)\n\n# Find the intervals where the function is monotonically decreasing\ndecreasing_intervals = sp.solve(sp.cos(2*x + sp.pi/6) < 0, x)\n\n# Find the minimum value of the function in the interval [0, pi/2]\na = sp.symbols(\'a\')\nf_min = 2 * sp.sin(2*x + sp.pi/6) + a\nmin_value = f_min.subs(sp.sin(2*x + sp.pi/6), -1)\na_value = sp.solve(min_value + 2, a)[0]\n\nprint(f"Simplified function: {f_simplified}")\nprint(f"Derivative: {f_prime}")\nprint(f"Intervals where the function is monotonically decreasing: {decreasing_intervals}")\nprint(f"Value of a: {a_value}")\n```\n```output\nSimplified function: a + 2*sin(2*x + pi/6)\nDerivative: 4*cos(2*x + pi/6)\nIntervals where the function is monotonically decreasing: (pi/6 < x) & (x < 2*pi/3)\nValue of a: 0\n```\nBased on the output from the Python code, we can summarize the results as follows:\n\n### Part I: Smallest Positive Period\nThe smallest positive period of the function \\(f(x) = \\sin \\left(2x + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\sin \\left(2x - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + \\cos 2x + a\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).\n\n### Part II: Intervals Where the Function is Monotonically Decreasing\nThe intervals where the function is monotonically decreasing are \\(\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)}\\).\n\n### Part III: Value of \\(a\\)\nIf the minimum value of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(-2\\) for \\(x \\in \\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}