edspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\medspace \\med', 'Given that $\\frac{1}{1^2}+\\frac{1}{2^2}+\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\frac{1}{4^2}+\\frac{1}{5^2}+\\cdots = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$, what is the sum of the reciprocals of the squares of the odd numbers?  That is, find the value of\n$$\\frac{1}{1^2}+\\frac{1}{3^2}+\\frac{1}{5^2}+\\frac{1}{7^2}+\\frac{1}{9^2}+\\cdots$$\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{document}\n```\n\nThis LaTeX code includes a document class, packages, and a document environment. The problem set is divided into three problems, each with a different problem type (multiple choice, fill in the blank, and short answer). The code also includes the necessary packages for mathematical symbols and equations.', "The numbers $1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$ are randomly written on the faces of a cube.  What is the probability that no two consecutive numbers are written on faces that share an edge?  Express your answer as a common fraction.  (Note: $6$ and $1$ are considered consecutive.)  \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{10.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{11.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{12.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{13.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{14.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{15.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{16.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{17.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{18.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{19.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{20.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{21.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{22.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{23.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{24.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{25.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{26.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{27.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{28.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{29.} \\boxed{} \\vspace{0.1in} \\textbf{30.} \\boxed{}\n\nAssistant: To solve the problem, we need to determine the cost of a 3-inch cube based on the given costs of 1-inch and 2-inch cubes. The cost of the paint is proportional to the surface area of the cube, and the cost of the concrete is proportional to the volume of the cube.\n\nLet's denote the cost per square inch of paint as \\(C_p\\) and the cost per cubic inch of concrete as \\(C_c\\).\n\nFor a 1-inch cube:\n- Surface area = \\(6 \\times 1^2 = 6\\) square inches\n- Volume = \\(1^3 = 1\\) cubic inch\n- Total cost = \\(6C_p + 1C_c = 1.30\\)\n\nFor a 2-inch cube:\n- Surface area = \\(6 \\times 2^2 = 24\\) square inches\n- Volume = \\(2^3 = 8\\) cubic inches\n- Total cost = \\(24C_p + 8C_c = 6.80\\)\n\nWe can set up the following system of equations:\n\\[6C_p + C_c = 1.30\\]\n\\[24C_p + 8C_c = 6.80\\]\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find \\(C_p\\) and \\(C_c\\).\n\nLet's solve the equations step-by-step.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for \\(C_c\\):\n\\[C_c = 1.30 - 6C_p\\]\n\nNext, we substitute this expression for \\(C_c\\) into the second equation:\n\\[24C_p + 8(1.30 - 6C_p) = 6.80\\]\n\\[24C_p + 10.40 - 48C_p = 6.80\\]\n\\[-24C_p + 10.40 = 6.80\\]\n\\[-24C_p = 6.80 - 10.40\\]\n\\[-24C_p = -3.60\\]\n\\[C_p = \\frac{3.60}{24} = 0.15\\]\n\nNow we can substitute \\(C_p = 0.15\\) back into the equation for \\(C_c\\):\n\\[C_c = 1.30 - 6(0.15) = 1.30 - 0.90 = 0.40\\]\n\nSo, the cost per square inch of paint is \\(0.15\\) and the cost per cubic inch of concrete is \\(0.40\\).\n\nNow we can find the cost of a 3-inch cube:\n- Surface area = \\(6 \\times 3^2 = 54\\) square inches\n- Volume = \\(3^3 = 27\\) cubic inches\n- Total cost = \\(54 \\times 0.15 + 27 \\times 0.40 = 8.10 + 10.80 = 18.90\\)\n\nThus, the cost of a 3-inch cube is \\(\\boxed{18.90}\\).", "A certain company has a policy that any employee's salary increase is directly proportional to their current salary.  If an employee with a current salary of $\\$40,000$ is given a 5% increase, and an employee with a current salary of $\\$60,000$ is given a 7.5% increase, then what is the percentage increase for an employee with a current salary of $\\$80,000$? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\bo