ow\nS_third_row = 2 + n + (n - 1)\n\n# Verify the value of S using the first column\nS_first_column = (n - 3) + (n + 2) + 2\n\n# Verify the value of S using the second column\nS_second_column = 3 + (2 * n - 9) + n\n\n# Verify the value of S using the third column\nS_third_column = (n + 1) + 1 + (n - 1)\n\n# Verify the value of S using the main diagonal\nS_main_diagonal = (n - 3) + (2 * n - 9) + (n - 1)\n\n# Verify the value of S using the other diagonal\nS_other_diagonal = (n + 1) + (2 * n - 9) + 2\n\n# Check if all values of S are equal\nprint(((S, S_second_row, S_third_row, S_first_column, S_second_column, S_third_column, S_main_diagonal, S_other_diagonal)))\n```\n```output\n(15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15, 15)\n```\nAll the values of \\( S \\) are equal to 15, confirming that the value of \\( n = 7 \\) is correct. Therefore, the value of \\( n \\) in the magic square is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the value of \\( n \\) such that the given 3x3 magic square has the same sum for each row, each column, and both diagonals.\n\nThe magic square is represented as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nLet's denote the common sum by \\( S \\). We can start by calculating the sum of the first row:\n\n\\[\nS = (n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = 2n - 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we can use the sum of the second row to verify \\( S \\):\n\n\\[\nS = (n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = 3n - 6\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions represent the same sum \\( S \\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 3n - 6\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 3n - 6 \\implies 6 - 1 = 3n - 2n \\implies n = 5\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this value by checking the sum of the columns and diagonals. Let's substitute \\( n = 5 \\) into the magic square and check the sums:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n2 & 3 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n7 & 1 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 5 & 4 \\\\\n