 (n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = S \\implies 2n + 1 = S \\]\n\n2. Sum of the second row:\n\\[ (n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = S \\implies 3n - 6 = S \\]\n\n3. Sum of the third row:\n\\[ 2 + n + (n-1) = S \\implies 2n + 1 = S \\]\n\n4. Sum of the first column:\n\\[ (n-3) + (n+2) + 2 = S \\implies 2n + 1 = S \\]\n\n5. Sum of the second column:\n\\[ 3 + (2n-9) + n = S \\implies 3n - 6 = S \\]\n\n6. Sum of the third column:\n\\[ (n+1) + 1 + (n-1) = S \\implies 2n + 1 = S \\]\n\n7. Sum of the main diagonal:\n\\[ (n-3) + (2n-9) + (n-1) = S \\implies 4n - 13 = S \\]\n\n8. Sum of the other diagonal:\n\\[ (n+1) + (2n-9) + 2 = S \\implies 3n - 6 = S \\]\n\nFrom the above equations, we can see that \\( S = 2n + 1 \\) from the first, third, fourth, and sixth rows/columns. We can also see that \\( S = 3n - 6 \\) from the second, fifth, and eighth rows/columns. Therefore, we can set these two expressions for \\( S \\) equal to each other:\n\\[ 2n + 1 = 3n - 6 \\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[ 2n + 1 = 3n - 6 \\implies 1 + 6 = 3n - 2n \\implies 7 = n \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( n \\) is \\( 7 \\). Let's verify this by substituting \\( n = 7 \\) back into the magic square and checking the sums.\n\nThe magic square with \\( n = 7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n4 & 3 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n9 & 5 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 7 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe sum of each row, each column, and the two main diagonals is \\( 15 \\), confirming that \\( n = 7 \\) is correct.\n\nThus, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To solve the magic square problem, we need to ensure that the sum of the numbers in each row, each column, and the two main diagonals are equal. Let's denote the common sum by \\( S \\).\n\nThe given magic square is:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we will write down the equations for the sums of the rows, columns, and diagonals.\n\n1. Sum of the first row:\n\\[\n(n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n2n + 1 = S\n\\]\n\n2. Sum of the second row:\n\\[\n(n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n3n - 6 = S\n\\]\n\n3. Sum of the third row:\n\\[\n2 + n + (n-1) = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n2n + 1 = S\n\\]\n\n4. Sum of the first column:\n\\[\n(n-3) + (n+2) + 2 = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n2n + 1 = S\n\\]\n\n5. Sum of the second column:\n\\[\n3 + (2n-9) + n = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n3n - 6 = S\n\\]\n\n6. Sum of the third column:\n\\[\n(n+1) + 1 + (n-1) = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n2n + 1 = S\n\\]\n\n7. Sum of the main diagonal (top-left to bottom-right):\n\\[\n(n-3) + (2n-9) + (n-1) = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n4n - 13 = S\n\\]\n\n8. Sum of the other diagonal (top-right to bottom-left):\n\\[\n(n+1) + (2n-9) + 2 = S\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n3n - 6 = S\n\\]\n\nSince all these sums must be equal to \\( S \\), we can equate any two of these expressions to find \\( n \\). Let's equate the expressions from the first row and the second row:\n\\[\n2n + 1 = 3n - 6\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[\n1 + 6 = 3n - 2n\n\\]\n\\[\n7 = n\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute \\( n = 7 \\) back into the magic square to verify the solution:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n4 & 3 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n9 & 5 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 7 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe common sum \\( S \\) is:\n\\[\n4 + 3 + 8 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n9 + 5 + 1 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n2 + 7 + 6 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n4 + 9 + 2 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n3 + 5 + 7 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n8 + 1 + 6 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n4 + 5 + 6 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\n8 + 5 + 2 = 15\n\\]\n\nSince all the sums are equal to 15, the solution is correct. Therefore, the value of \\( n \\) is \\( \\boxed{7} \\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\( n \\) such that the given 3x3 grid forms a magic square. A magic square is a grid where the sum of the numbers in each row, each column, and each of the two main diagonals is the same.