ible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) (with the leading coefficient factored out) is given by \\( -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n} \\). In this case, the sum of the roots is \\( -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2} \\). The product of the roots is given by \\( (-1)^n \\frac{a_0}{a_n} = (-1)^n \\frac{66}{2} = (-1)^n \\cdot 33 \\). Since the roots are distinct integers, we need to find a set of distinct integers whose product is \\( \\pm 33 \\).\n\nThe integer factorizations of 33 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 33 \\), \\( \\pm 3, \\pm 11 \\), and \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 11 \\). We need to find a combination of these factors that gives the smallest possible value for \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nLet's consider the possible sets of roots and calculate the corresponding value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\):\n\n1. Roots: \\( 1, -1, 33, -33 \\) (for \\( n = 4 \\))\n2. Roots: \\( 1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11 \\) (for \\( n = 6 \\))\n3. Roots: \\( 1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 1, -1 \\) (for \\( n = 8 \\))\n\nWe need to find the combination that gives the smallest \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nLet's calculate the possible values of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) for these sets of roots.\n\n1. For roots \\( 1, -1, 33, -33 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_{n-1} = -2(1 + (-1) + 33 + (-33)) = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\implies |a_{n-1}| = 0\n   \\]\n2. For roots \\( 1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_{n-1} = -2(1 + (-1) + 3 + (-3) + 11 + (-11)) = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\implies |a_{n-1}| = 0\n   \\]\n3. For roots \\( 1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11, 1, -1 \\):\n   \\[\n   a_{n-1} = -2(1 + (-1) + 3 + (-3) + 11 + (-11) + 1 + (-1)) = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\implies |a_{n-1}| = 0\n   \\]\n\nWe see that in all cases, the value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is 0. However, we need to ensure that the polynomial has distinct integer roots, and the above sets of roots are not valid because they do not provide distinct roots when considering the product \\( \\pm 33 \\).\n\nLet's consider the next smallest possible set of distinct roots whose product is 