e function is \\( 2 < x < 102 \\) excluding \\( x = 12 \\). In interval notation, this is written as \\( (2, 12) \\cup (12, 102) \\).\n\nThus, the domain of the function is \\(\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}\\).", "To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. This involves ensuring that the denominator is not zero and that the argument of the logarithm is positive.\n\n1. The argument of the outer logarithm must be positive:\n   \\[\n   2 - \\log(x-2) > 0 \\implies \\log(x-2) < 2 \\implies x-2 < 10^2 \\implies x-2 < 100 \\implies x < 102.\n   \\]\n   Additionally, since \\( \\log(x-2) \\) is defined, we need \\( x-2 > 0 \\implies x > 2 \\).\n\n2. The argument of the inner logarithm must be positive:\n   \\[\n   x-2 > 0 \\implies x > 2.\n   \\]\n\n3. The denominator must not be zero:\n   \\[\n   \\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\neq 0 \\implies 2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies x-2 \\neq 10 \\implies x \\neq 12.\n   \\]\n\nCombining these conditions, the domain of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( 2 < x < 102 \\) excluding \\( x = 12 \\). In interval notation, this is:\n\\[\n(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102).\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = (2 - x) / sp.log(2 - sp.log(x - 2, 10), 10)\n\n# Find the domain\ndomain = sp.calculus.util.continuous_domain(f, x, sp.S.Reals)\nprint(domain)\n```\n```output\nUnion(Interval.open(2, 12), Interval.open(12, 102))\n```\nThe domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\) is indeed \\( (2, 12) \\cup (12, 102) \\).\n\nSo, the final answer in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}\n\\]", 'Given $z = 1+i$, we can calculate $w$ as follows:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{3z+1}{5z+7} = \\frac{3(1+i)+1}{5(1+i)+7} = \\frac{3+3i+1}{5+5i+7} = \\frac{4+3i}{12+5i}\n\\]\nTo find $|w|$, we first simplify $w$:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{4+3i}{12+5i} \\cdot \\frac{12-5i}{12-5i} = \\frac{48-20i+36i-15i^2}{144-25i^2} = \\frac{63+16i}{169}\n\\]\nSince $i^2 = -1$, we have:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{63+16i}{169}\n\\]\nNow, to find $|w|$, we calculate the magnitude of $w$:\n\\[\n|w| = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{63}{169}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{16}{169}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3969+256}{28561}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{28561}} = \\frac{65}{169}\n\\]\nThus, the magnitude of $w$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{65}{169}}$.', "To find \\( |w| \\) where \\( w = \\dfrac{3z + 1}{5z + 7} \\) and \\( z = 1 + i \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( z = 1 + i \\) into the expression for \\( w \\).\n2. Simplify the expression to get \\( w \\) in the form \\( a + bi \\).\n3. Calculate the magnitude \\( |w| \\) using the formula \\( |w| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nLet's go through these steps using Python code.\n\nFirst, we'll substitute \\( z = 1 + i \\) into the expression for \\( w \\):\n\n\\[ w = \\dfrac{3(1 + i) + 1}{5(1 + i) + 7} = \\dfrac{3 + 3i + 1}{5 + 5i + 7} = \\dfrac{4 + 3i}{12 + 5i}. \\]\n\nNext, we'll simplify this expression by multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n\\[ w = \\dfrac{(4 + 3i)(12 - 5i)}{(12 + 5i)(12 - 5i)} = \\dfrac{48 - 20i + 36i - 15i^2}{144 - 25i^2} = \\dfrac{48 + 16i + 15}{144 + 25} = \\dfrac{63 + 16i}{169} = \\dfrac{63}{169} + \\dfrac{16}{169}i. \\]\n\nNow we can calculate the magnitude \\( |w| \\):\n\n\\[ |w| = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{63}{169}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{16}{169}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{63^2 + 16^2}{169^2}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3969 + 256}{169^2}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4225}{169^2}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4225}{28561}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{25}{169}} = \\dfrac{5}{13}. \\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\n\n# Define the complex number z\nz = 1 + 1j\n\n# Calculate w\nw = (3*z + 1) / (5*z + 7)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of w\nmagnitude_w = abs(w)\nprint(magnitude_