n# Area of one right triangle\narea_triangle = (1/2) * (math.sqrt(2) / 2) * 1\n\n# Total area of the triangles\ntotal_area_triangles = 4 * area_triangle\n\n# Area of the octagon\narea_octagon = area_rectangle - total_area_triangles\nprint(area_octagon)\n```\n```output\n9.899494936611665\n```\nThe calculated area of the octagon is approximately $9.899494936611665$, which is equivalent to $7\\sqrt{2}$. Therefore, the exact area of the octagon is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]", "We can split the octagon into a square with side length $\\sqrt{2}$ and four isosceles right triangles with legs of length $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The area of the square is $2$, and the area of each triangle is $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$. Therefore, the total area of the octagon is $2 + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\boxed{3}$.\n\nIn an angle-angle-side triangle, the angles are $\\frac{11 \\pi }{30}$ and $\\frac{7 \\pi }{45}$, and one side has a length of $9$ units. Find the coordinates of the triangle's vertices and the measures of its three interior angles. The angles of the triangle are as follows:\n1. $\\frac{11 \\pi }{30}$\n2. $\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi }{90}\\right)-\\sin\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi }{45}\\right)}{\\sqrt{2+\\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi }{30}\\right)}}\\right)$\n3. $\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\cos\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi }{45}\\right)-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\right)}{\\sqrt{2+\\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi }{30}\\right)}}\\right)$\n\nThe vertices of the triangle are located at:\n1. $\\{0,0\\}$ (origin)\n2. $\\left\\{9 \\cos\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi }{90}\\right) \\sec\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi }{15}\\right), 0\\right\\}$\n3. $\\left\\{9 \\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi }{45}\\right) \\tan\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi }{15}\\right), 9 \\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi }{45}\\right)\\right\\}$\n\nThese revised versions maintain the necessary information and are presented in a more concise and coherent manner. The answer has been checked for accuracy and 